{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1452173-cultural-appropriation-in-gaming","id":1452173,"title":"Cultural Appropriation in gaming","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness"],"posts":[{"date":"2021-06-14T08:58:14-07:00","id":1452173,"title":"Cultural Appropriation in gaming","content":"This is something I've offered up for discussions with other systems, and it is rarely met with a warm reception.<br /> Still, I would like some input on the subject: how do you deal with the concept that many common tropes of horror and fantasy come from real-world cultures, and how do you navigate/mitigate the consequences?<br /> <br /> I think WWG and OPP have a brilliant model in the lists upon lists of sources of inspiration in their books. It basically says &quot;this isn't our thing, originally. And here are some of the places where we heard about it and their different takes on it and how that has shaped our perception of it.&quot; I really like that a lot.<br /> <br /> To be clear, I'm not talking about like...golems and werewolves, the mythologies that have been well and truly relegated to the history books and to legend. Those guys are all ready and waiting to be popped into one Monster Manual or another. That seems fine to me.<br /> It's like...specifically cultural beliefs like the wendigo and the skin-walker. Stories that are very much a part of a living system, and ones that members of said culture have explicitly asked others not to appropriate/adapt/change/spread.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26844-quixote","name":"Quixote","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"40"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-14T11:36:10-07:00","id":1452190,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Quixote</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1452173-cultural-appropriation-in-gaming#post1452173\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">This is something I've offered up for discussions with other systems, and it is rarely met with a warm reception.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think a lot of people struggle with the concept of cultural appropriation vs. health cultural exchange. On one hand it is simple in theory, but on the other is quiet complicated in real world practice.<br /> <br /> There's a knee-jerk reaction to the concept because pretty much everyone benefits from and enjoys media and culture that is born of some amount of cultural appropriation; and thus people don't want to be &quot;bad&quot; so they reject the underlying concept rather than reevaluating things. Making it a hard topic to bring up in general.<br /> <br /> Threads on the forums here about it in the past haven't always gone well.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> how do you deal with the concept that many common tropes of horror and fantasy come from real-world cultures, and how do you navigate/mitigate the consequences? </div> </div> </div>Do my best to keep myself educated; other people don't have a responsibility to teach me why something is disrespectful.<br /> Listen to my group to see what they need/want to enjoy the game; I'm not writing stuff for general consumption, what matters is my friends having fun.<br /> Do my best to speak up against problematic comments and behaviors; making my gaming spaces fun places to be, means making sure I take on my share of the emotional work of establishing what's acceptable.<br /> Acknowledge that no matter how much work I do, I will make mistakes; people that care don't begrudge honest mistakes, they just want you to do the best you can.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I think WWG and OPP have a brilliant model in the lists upon lists of sources of inspiration in their books. </div> </div> </div>It's nice, but it's not always great for this specific topic. Inspiration sections can also be telling that the authors didn't really research the topic as well as they should have and are just aping pop-culture and spread the appropriation further.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> To be clear, I'm not talking about like...golems and werewolves, the mythologies that have been well and truly relegated to the history books and to legend. Those guys are all ready and waiting to be popped into one Monster Manual or another. That seems fine to me.<br /> It's like...specifically cultural beliefs like the wendigo and the skin-walker. Stories that are very much a part of a living system, and ones that members of said culture have explicitly asked others not to appropriate/adapt/change/spread. </div> </div> </div>.... so.... speaking of mistakes and doing better...<br /> <br /> Don't be fine with pop-culture golems please. Golems are part of my living cultural system. Lots of Jewish creatives in the fantasy and TTRPG spheres have asked people to stop using &quot;golem&quot; to mean any sort of magical construct.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":9}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-14T12:49:47-07:00","id":1452206,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1452173-cultural-appropriation-in-gaming?p=1452190#post1452190\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">(a lot of valuable and salient points)</div> </div> </div> </div>I would agree on all points, really. I've met people who have seen me trying my best and still weren't satisfied/were still upset with me when I erred, but at that point I just don't know what they actually wanted from me or if it's reasonable.<br /> <br /> Re: golems. Wow, okay. That is an eye-opener. The discussions I've seen on the subject thus far seemed to place those stories in the same category as, say, angels, demons and d'jinn. That is, that they are a part of the cultural heritage, but not a living, breathing part. Thank you for bringing this to my attention. I'm definitely going to look into it more now.<br /> With that said, how does PtC handle the subject , in your opinion?<br /> <br /> Also, while I'm all for playing to your audience, that still leaves me wondering how you build a game/setting meant for the general public and avoid these same pitfalls?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26844-quixote","name":"Quixote","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"40"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-14T16:02:16-07:00","id":1452247,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Quixote</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1452173-cultural-appropriation-in-gaming?p=1452206#post1452206\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">II've met people who have seen me trying my best and still weren't satisfied/were still upset with me when I erred, but at that point I just don't know what they actually wanted from me or if it's reasonable.</div> </div> </div> </div>Depending on the subject? They don't want anything from you in particular. Some things are just too painful for some people for there to be a nuanced approach that satisfies their emotional needs and your artistic/etc. desires.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The discussions I've seen on the subject thus far seemed to place those stories in the same category as, say, angels, demons and d'jinn. </div> </div> </div>Angels as commonly pictured in mainstream western culture have almost nothing to do with the angels of my culture. They're an amalgamation of things that do come from my religion, but blended together it half a dozen other religions/cultures.<br /> <br /> Demons as a reflection of angels aren't part of my religion/culture. The angel/demon thing is far more a Zoroastrian influence.<br /> <br /> D'jinn are definitely not part of my culture.<br /> <br /> So I prefer not to speak too strongly on how to approach them and appropriation. My personal view is that the Christian conception of angels and demons are a distinct and uniquely separate thing partially inspired by Judaism, and thus aren't really appropriation. There is also the complicated issue of Christianity being a valid claimant to Jewish cultural elements, but also guilty of appropriation, cultural erasure, and so on. They're hard things to draw lines around. What makes golems different is that they weren't something that filtered into Christian theology and mythology, and thus became part of mainstream western culture with Christianity's rise to power. Golems remained a uniquely Jewish cultural expression until very recently, and with very little respect for Jewish culture in the process of it being a mainstream pop-culture element.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> With that said, how does PtC handle the subject , in your opinion? </div> </div> </div>Fine.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Also, while I'm all for playing to your audience, that still leaves me wondering how you build a game/setting meant for the general public and avoid these same pitfalls? </div> </div> </div>I think golems in the CofD are a good example.<br /> <br /> You immediately jump to PtC, which is a fine game for exploring playing a golem struggling with being a created entity. But does a golem have to be a Promethean? Not at all.<br /> <br /> If you can imagine, easily, a Promethean, a Deviant, a mage's creation, an spirit possessing a body, and a weird Infrastructure event all calling themselves a golem with respect to the Jewish cultural expression that term applies, I think you've taken the right approach to building a game setting that's in the kitchen-sink arena (doing a far more detailed story would be a different matter). Keep the player characters unique to the setting, that can express a cultural story, or not. Blank slates can have any story you want written on them.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-14T16:36:54-07:00","id":1452252,"title":"","content":"It somewhat helps that, at least in the CofD, most of the time a supernatural claiming a name means exactly that; <i>claiming</i>, not <i>being</i>. An American mage of the Devourers of the Flesh Legacy calling himself a Wendigo does not mean that \u2018true\u2019 Wendigos are those Legacy members (and thus that Native American beliefs are wrong).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>MtAw Homebrew:<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1051456-even-more-legacies-updated-to-2e#post1051456\" target=\"_blank\">Even more Legacies, updated to 2E</a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1443819-new-2e-legacies-expanded\" target=\"_blank\">New 2E Legacies, expanded</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12619-21c-hermit","name":"21C Hermit","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12619_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3489"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-14T18:37:36-07:00","id":1452261,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1452173-cultural-appropriation-in-gaming?p=1452247#post1452247\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Depending on the subject? They don't want anything from you in particular. Some things are just too painful for some people for there to be a nuanced approach that satisfies their emotional needs and your artistic/etc. desires.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> Angels as commonly pictured in mainstream western culture have almost nothing to do with the angels of my culture. They're an amalgamation of things that do come from my religion, but blended together it half a dozen other religions/cultures.<br /> <br /> Demons as a reflection of angels aren't part of my religion/culture. The angel/demon thing is far more a Zoroastrian influence.<br /> <br /> D'jinn are definitely not part of my culture.<br /> <br /> So I prefer not to speak too strongly on how to approach them and appropriation. My personal view is that the Christian conception of angels and demons are a distinct and uniquely separate thing partially inspired by Judaism, and thus aren't really appropriation. There is also the complicated issue of Christianity being a valid claimant to Jewish cultural elements, but also guilty of appropriation, cultural erasure, and so on. They're hard things to draw lines around. What makes golems different is that they weren't something that filtered into Christian theology and mythology, and thus became part of mainstream western culture with Christianity's rise to power. Golems remained a uniquely Jewish cultural expression until very recently, and with very little respect for Jewish culture in the process of it being a mainstream pop-culture element.<br /> <br /> <br /> Fine.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> I think golems in the CofD are a good example.<br /> <br /> You immediately jump to PtC, which is a fine game for exploring playing a golem struggling with being a created entity. But does a golem have to be a Promethean? Not at all.<br /> <br /> If you can imagine, easily, a Promethean, a Deviant, a mage's creation, an spirit possessing a body, and a weird Infrastructure event all calling themselves a golem with respect to the Jewish cultural expression that term applies, I think you've taken the right approach to building a game setting that's in the kitchen-sink arena (doing a far more detailed story would be a different matter). Keep the player characters unique to the setting, that can express a cultural story, or not. Blank slates can have any story you want written on them.</div> </div> </div> </div>I Liked this. Very elegantly said.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"","name":"Guest","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/default/default_avatar_large.png","title":"","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-15T10:06:53-07:00","id":1452348,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1452173-cultural-appropriation-in-gaming?p=1452247#post1452247\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Depending on the subject? They don't want anything from you in particular. Some things are just too painful for some people for there to be a nuanced approach that satisfies their emotional needs and your artistic/etc. desires.</div> </div> </div> </div>That is a very generous and amiable approach to what I'm sure most people write off as &quot;difficult people&quot;.<br /> I don't always side with the offended party; I do think that some people simply look for things to take offense to. But I think the inherent validity of everyone's thoughts and feelings expressed in your point is extremely important for the gaming community and in general.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1452173-cultural-appropriation-in-gaming?p=1452247#post1452247\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">There is also the complicated issue of Christianity being a valid claimant to Jewish cultural elements, but also guilty of appropriation, cultural erasure, and so on. They're hard things to draw lines around. What makes golems different is that they weren't something that filtered into Christian theology and mythology, and thus became part of mainstream western culture with Christianity's rise to power. Golems remained a uniquely Jewish cultural expression until very recently, and with very little respect for Jewish culture in the process of it being a mainstream pop-culture element.</div> </div> </div> </div>Ouphe. Yeah, those are some muddy waters. But that makes sense, how the golem stories haven't been filtered and adapted into the mainstream.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>21C Hermit</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1452173-cultural-appropriation-in-gaming?p=1452252#post1452252\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">...An American mage of the Devourers of the Flesh Legacy calling himself a Wendigo does not mean that \u2018true\u2019 Wendigos are those Legacy members (and thus that Native American beliefs are wrong).</div> </div> </div> </div> And that is an elegant approach to the whole dilemma.<br /> The problem with that specific example is that there are people who hold those stories to be part of their cultural identity who have outright said they do not want people outside of that culture to use those stories, or even talk about them.<br /> <br /> I don't know if there's a way to keep everyone happy, or if a 100% hands-off approach is even a reasonable model.<br /> Even after all the research I did, I still have a story about skin-walkers I'd really like to tell. And I *think* my approach would be respectful and accurate enough to avoid causing actual harm to the community that the story belongs to. But...yeah. Still apprehensive, of course.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26844-quixote","name":"Quixote","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"40"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-15T11:44:40-07:00","id":1452361,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26844-quixote\" style=\"background-image:url('data:image/svg+xml;base64,');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"26844\" data-vbnamecard=\"26844\">Quixote</a> Don't overextend yourself to the point of a breakdown just to please everyone that takes issue with what you create. You cannot address potentially valid complaints if you are so stressed out that you can't think straight. Pay attention to those who come to you in earnest, and ignore all the rest. Because those who take offense in a manufactured and malicious way will never, ever be pleased with anything you do, or even who you are.<br /> <br /> I wish you a lot of wisdom so you can reliably tell the difference between people who are worth listening to, and people who are just driven by spite. As long as you create without the desire to harm and with effort to be mindful, then your critics should likewise be patient and understanding. I'm obviously speaking from an idealistic perspective, such a balanced dynamic is something to strive for rather than something that can be established permanently. But don't let that discourage you from creating amazing art, of course. The world would be even more of a dreary and bleak place if all artists gave up in the face of adversity and error.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Bye.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4443-gibberingeloquence","name":"GibberingEloquence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4443_5.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2470"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-15T14:26:47-07:00","id":1452378,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Quixote</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1452173-cultural-appropriation-in-gaming?p=1452348#post1452348\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">That is a very generous and amiable approach to what I'm sure most people write off as &quot;difficult people&quot;.</div> </div> </div> </div>I get written off as a difficult person a lot... probably helps my ability to put myself in those shoes. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I don't always side with the offended party; I do think that some people simply look for things to take offense to. </div> </div> </div>I really don't feel this is a useful way to approach things. I've never seen, &quot;you were looking for things to take offense to,&quot; ever help people understand each other.<br /> <br /> What I try to assume is a slightly different thing: The work in question set off red flags for the person, and they read it with all the preconceptions that entails. It's not an active process. Confirmation bias is something we all do, because we're wired to do so. When you see it as not people actively trying to be offended, but rather people falling back on mental defenses they've built up for reasons, you have a better path forward to discuss a potential way through that communication log-jam.<br /> <br /> For example: When I see modern Americans use the term&quot;Judeo-Christian,&quot; esp. in a political context, it's a huge red flag for me. I automatically assume they really mean, &quot;White evangelical Christian dressed up as either being the true form of all Abrahamic faith, or just trying to make one particularly view point sound far more inclusive than it is.&quot; Why? Because far, far too often that's exactly what I end up dealing with. It's offensive to me before I even get into the actual discussion, because there is a long - still active - history of Christians using their connection to Judaism as a shield or excuse, and then ignoring Jews and Jewish thought on the topics. Esp. in the social media era, I'm constantly aware of people that treat me, my people, and my faith as a prop for them to score points with, and disagree our actual feelings and beliefs.<br /> <br /> Intellectually, I know not everyone that's talking about Judeo-Christian whatever is actually falling into that behavior. But once something primes me to see that pattern, a pattern I've seen repeated more times than days I've been alive, it's a lot of emotional work on my end to not see the inklings of that pattern and assume that the full thing is going to emerge if I keep engaging.<br /> <br /> It's something like when someone says, &quot;I don't want to sound racist, but...&quot; and then whatever they say sounds more racist for it. Imagine someone saying, &quot;I don't want to sound racist, but strawberry ice-cream is clearly the best flavor.&quot; It's silly, but you can't help but wonder, &quot;wait, what to strawberries have to do with racism?&quot; You start trying to figure out the connection, because it doesn't make sense and we want the world to make sense. That's what &quot;looking for offense,&quot; really starts with.<br /> <br /> I've seen countless discussion break down because the person taking offense is, frequently unstated, primed to be offended at the base premise/introduction of the topic and is seeing justifications for it as thy continue, while those trying to defend the topic aren't seeing the initial issue, and they try to defend the work/discussion with counter-examples or in-depth nuanced analysis. It doesn't work because it never addresses that opening issue.<br /> <br /> Of course, there's always people that aren't actually offended, and are stating offense for other motivations. Ignoring those though, if you feel someone is genuinely offended, but seems like they're &quot;looking&quot; for it, and you want to try to get past that? Start with the red flags, not details. Figure out why they're in confirmation bias mode, because seeing that perspective is the only way you can state your side of things in a fashion they'll listen to. As they say, meet people where they are.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The problem with that specific example is that there are people who hold those stories to be part of their cultural identity who have outright said they do not want people outside of that culture to use those stories, or even talk about them. </div> </div> </div>It's important to remember that no community is a monolith. While there are cultures that have secret insider knowledge that's not supposed to be shared with outsiders, there's a lot more people that are just trying to reclaim agency over their own culture rather than keep things hidden from everyone else. Just because I ask people to not be OK with pop-culture golems doesn't mean every Jew feels that way, or it even represents a majority opinion of Jews. Maybe I'm just a stick in the mud and offending me isn't really that big of a deal for anything meant for general consumption instead of something just for a theoretical gaming group we're both in.<br /> <br /> Calls for outsiders to stop using those cultural elements is largely because it's so hard to reclaim those things after being appropriated. Getting outsiders to stop is the only way to get the breathing room for insiders to have a shot.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I don't know if there's a way to keep everyone happy, or if a 100% hands-off approach is even a reasonable model. </div> </div> </div>There's no way, and thus it is not reasonable, for anything meant for public consumption.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Even after all the research I did, I still have a story about skin-walkers I'd really like to tell. And I *think* my approach would be respectful and accurate enough to avoid causing actual harm to the community that the story belongs to. But...yeah. Still apprehensive, of course. </div> </div> </div>My advice in this particular regard is:<br /> <br /> 1) Reach out to people like sensitivity readers that specialize in a given culture. Figure out what you can afford obviously (that's work they're doing, don't expect freebies), but do you best to get insider opinions on how respectful and non-harmful it is, rather than assuming. It might hurt to get negative responses, but you know. And if you get positive responses it says a lot about how much work you did.<br /> <br /> 2) Some stories aren't ours to tell. It's good to accept that, and accepting that reduces a lot of apprehension. There's 8 billion people on Earth, you have lots of stories to tell, to lots of people that want to hear them. Letting some neat ideas get away because you're not the right people to tell them just means you're helping make room for the right person to tell them to do so. You still learned a lot of interesting things, expanded your perspective, and grew as a person in the process of ending up with a story that you decide you shouldn't' do.<br /> <br /> 3) If you go through with it, you do your best, and you take your lumps from people that don't think its good enough in stride. All art has haters. Lots of haters have a point. It sucks, it's painful, but in the end you can find way to do better next time. There's no perfect art, there's just better art than your previous work, and we all need that critical eye on our work to get to better work.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":9}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-15T17:56:09-07:00","id":1452397,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1452173-cultural-appropriation-in-gaming?p=1452378#post1452378\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">For example: When I see modern Americans use the term&quot;Judeo-Christian,&quot; esp. in a political context, it's a huge red flag for me. I automatically assume they really mean, &quot;White evangelical Christian dressed up as either being the true form of all Abrahamic faith, or just trying to make one particularly view point sound far more inclusive than it is.&quot; Why? Because far, far too often that's exactly what I end up dealing with. It's offensive to me before I even get into the actual discussion, because there is a long - still active - history of Christians using their connection to Judaism as a shield or excuse, and then ignoring Jews and Jewish thought on the topics. Esp. in the social media era, I'm constantly aware of people that treat me, my people, and my faith as a prop for them to score points with, and disagree our actual feelings and beliefs.</div> </div> </div> </div>Same, and often times when I point out why the phrase is problematic (and inherently anti-Semitic) the other party dismisses it out-of-hand, or accuses me of not really being Jewish, or decides that because &lt;insert Christian Evangelical&gt; used it the phrase is fine and I'm being histrionic.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1452235-proin-s-legacy-hub\" target=\"_blank\">proin's Legacy hub</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2757-proindrakenzol","name":"proindrakenzol","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2757_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1514"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-15T18:35:37-07:00","id":1452402,"title":"","content":"Yeah. Not to hijack the thread, but something I think can be indicative of these sorts of red flags rather than just harping on that to a receptive audience:<br /> <br /> The terms &quot;Judeo-Christian&quot; and &quot;Abrahamic,&quot; have a history of switching with each other in which is seen as &quot;respectful,&quot; vs. &quot;problematic.&quot; Outside of the specific context of comparative religious studies though, there's no real good reason to try to generalize Christianity and Judaism, or Christianity, Islam, and Judaism like that. Jews complain about how whichever phrase is popular at that time being used to erase the Jewish part of things, and then what changes is the word, not the message. The motivations leading to appropriation never get addressed, the window dressing gets changed.<br /> <br /> This is the kind of things to avoid doing when you're thinking about, &quot;how can I do better at using other culture's expressions respectfully.&quot; Getting caught up in the surface aspects, and not looking at what's really causing the upset is the kind of things that minorities esp. have long memories around.<br /> <br /> It's like all the companies that change their logos to rainbow colors for Pride, but then go back to giving money to anti-LGBTQ+ organizations and politicians. If you only put in the surface effort, and never address the underlying issues with what you're doing, you'll be seen, and the response isn't going to be good. When I talk about being accepted despite our mistakes, this is the contrast: when people don't see it as a mistake, just a play.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-15T23:35:33-07:00","id":1452421,"title":"","content":"Okay, less serious example but still on theme.<br /> I m italian and (surprise surprise) i love pizza.<br /> Sometimes i sees other cultures people (mostly americans) that tries to emulate Pizzas, with let s say mixed results.<br /> In those cases i m not offended, clearly they tried their best but they are not familiar with the original thing.<br /> But someone creates monstrous abominations that completely subverts the original concept, but they are foolish enough to still use the name Pizza.<br /> My soul literally bleeds every time.<br /> I m not saying that they cannot take a recipe and radically change it, but the NAME, it belong to us, we created it.<br /> I would not be offended if instead they call it Pallizza or Picago, i would feel it as &quot;i ll use this as an inspiration to create my own thing&quot; instead of &quot;i ll take your child as mine and torture it&quot;.<br /> I hope i was able to convey the message without seeming too silly.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24346-neos01","name":"Neos01","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24346_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1021"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-16T04:41:43-07:00","id":1452448,"title":"","content":"@Heavy Arms<br /> <br /> What do you think of this?<br /> <br /> My Jewish (Israeli, actually) friend told me that he tried pepperoni pizza once when he was younger and liked it even though he\u2019s always kept kosher. He said the reason he did that is cause it\u2019s not really a sacrifice to give it up if you don\u2019t know what you\u2019re missing.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"","name":"Guest","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/default/default_avatar_large.png","title":"","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-16T06:45:30-07:00","id":1452464,"title":"","content":"Some stuff has become a bit too blatantly used, but in regards to skin-walkers in particular, I understand, the Navajo nation does not like discussing them with outsiders. And as such it feels wrong for me to use in my games. I've also started to get a bit more sensitive when I see Native American culture being reflavored as mysticism within media. And man are there a lot of Urban Fantasy books where one of the main characters is a Native American who can shapeshift or has a spirit animal.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"b-bbcode b-bbcode__url b-bbcode__url--preview\"> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Skin-walker\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"urlimg_container\"> <img class=\"js-img-fallback\" src=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/static/images/icons/enwiki-25.svg\"> </a> <div class=\"urlinfo\"> <div class=\"title ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Skin-walker\" target=\"_blank\"> Skin-walker - Wikipedia </a> </div> <div class=\"url ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Skin-walker\" target=\"_blank\"> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Skin-walker </a> </div> <div class=\"meta\"> </div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> That being said some times I'll take inspiration from folklore, but I'll not do a 1 to 1 representation and I'll not use the name.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25291-donteatrawhagis","name":"DontEatRawHagis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25291_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"211"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-16T08:07:20-07:00","id":1452481,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>GibberingEloquence</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1452173-cultural-appropriation-in-gaming?p=1452361#post1452361\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><span class=\"b-bbcode-user js-bbcode-user\">Guest</span> Don't overextend yourself to the point of a breakdown just to please everyone that takes issue with what you create.</div> </div> </div> </div> Oh, I don't think there's any risk of that. A disproportionate sense of worry/anxiety on my end, I mean.<br /> It's a complicated situation and can quickly lead to me talking myself in circles, but I assure you I'm not losing sleep over it.<br /> With that said though, I feel like my privilege and my perspective are such that I can afford to make myself a little uncomfortable on occasion.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1452173-cultural-appropriation-in-gaming?p=1452378#post1452378\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I really don't feel this is a useful way to approach things. I've never seen, &amp;quot;you were looking for things to take offense to,&amp;quot; ever help people understand each other.</div> </div> </div> </div>To be fair, I would never approach someone in that manner, even if part of me feels that way.<br /> I've had to deal with people who seemed to be bullies who hid their intent under the guise of offense; people who seemed to be waiting in the wings for me to &quot;slip up&quot; and then taking great delight in trying to nail me to the wall for it.<br /> Stand up for what you believe, correct others when they are mistaken or ignorant. That's all fine. But if they can't be kind or even civil, especially after they've received an apology and the other person has attempted to bridge the gap between them, I will no longer feel bad about upsetting them.<br /> I will still do my best to view the subject matter with a cool head and figure out if and how I was wrong, but I will not let their crap behavior weigh me down.<br /> <br /> But everything you said about confirmation biases rings true. The times I have actually had to draw the line have been few.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>DontEatRawHagis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1452173-cultural-appropriation-in-gaming?p=1452464#post1452464\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Native American culture being reflavored as mysticism within media.</div> </div> </div> </div>This, big time.<br /> In your typical Sword and Sorcery, fantasy-type stories, you see non-European cultures portrayed as mysterious, exotic and magical.<br /> <br /> But then, it's not like White Knights in shining armor slaying dragons or wise old Wizards with long, snowy beards in their towers were any real part of European life, either. But then-then, the filter you're invited to view the world from definitely seems to be a white, European one.<br /> <br /> I have to say, this thread has gone a million times better than my previous attempt. I had people PM'ing me to ask why I was &quot;leaning so hard into White Guilt&quot; and people commenting with &quot;cultural appropriation isn't real&quot;. So. Thank you all for this.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":26844,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26844-quixote","user":"Quixote","date":". 06-16-2021, 11:20 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26844-quixote","name":"Quixote","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"40"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-16T11:16:50-07:00","id":1452509,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Penelope</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1452173-cultural-appropriation-in-gaming?p=1452448#post1452448\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">What do you think of this?</div> </div> </div> </div>Just in general, it's frequently perceived as rude to ask one member of a minority to &quot;answer&quot; for the actions of another. As I said before, we're not a monolith. Judaism, more like an ethnic group than many religious groups, has no centralized leadership. There's no head rabbi dictating what is, and isn't appropriate Jewish behavior. The only religious authorities Jews recognize are: parents until their child is acknowledged as an adult by religious standards (the Bar Mitzvah ceremony), and the rabbi(s) an individual Jew chooses to follow (this is actually something that extends past religion in Jewish philosophy; Jews are expected to follow the advice of experts when they ask experts for advice, and thus are also told to be careful in picking their experts).<br /> <br /> So... while I think I get where you're coming from, please try to think of better ways to go about these sorts of questions (and this doesn't just apply to Jews).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> My Jewish (Israeli, actually) friend told me that he tried pepperoni pizza once when he was younger and liked it even though he\u2019s always kept kosher. He said the reason he did that is cause it\u2019s not really a sacrifice to give it up if you don\u2019t know what you\u2019re missing. </div> </div> </div>So, I'll address two things here. First is my best attempt to relate Jewish theological philosophy to the situation. Second, I'll address this as, &quot;HA, if you saw this character in a work, how would it make you feel?&quot;<br /> <br /> The first one is going to get a bit lengthy, so I'll start with the short version: While Judaism would consider this a transgression, and thus it would require ritual cleansing of the body and the soul to avoid negative divine judgement, most Jewish philosophers would say that it's more important to value the commandments of our faith from a place of wisdom than one of ignorance. It's better to transgress and know what not keeping Kosher is like if that helps you appreciate Kashrut, than to follow the laws by rote and have no real attachment to them.<br /> <br /> In Jewish moral philosophy, there are only three transgressions that have no exceptions. That is, every other rule in Judaism comes with caveats, and exists in a hierarchy of levels of transgressions, mitigating factors, and so on. It's important to note that Judaism does not phrase these three transgressions as inherently ones that come with specific punishments at mortal hands (though we do that too), but rather these are the three sins that you should die before you commit: murder, sexual violence, and idolatry (not going to get into that one in detail, but this is its own very complicated subject).<br /> <br /> So, where does keeping Kosher fit into all this? Surprisingly low actually. As long as it's just between you and the Lord, eating something unkosher is ultimately a fairly low down sin. There is no case where, if you had to pick between two transgressions, eating something unkosher isn't the clear choice to make. Even when it wouldn't be clear, it's a coin-flip between two equally less important ones. Remember, this is the maximum punishment for breaking these laws (again, just for yourself): say a prayer and take a ritual bath. That's all you have to do before you can reclaim your position as a Jew-in-good-standing to participate in Jewish ritual life. You can see why the rabbis do not think harshly of exploration of transgressions on this level in younger Jews.<br /> <br /> Of course, things get more complicated when other people get involved. Taking your impressionable younger sibling to your pepperoni pizza party is not OK. Rabbis and other prominent Jews are held to a higher standard lest their actions led others astray by creating a sense that it is not a transgression (however minor of one). And so on.<br /> <br /> And, to come from the other part of this, Judaism has a long standing internal debate over two seemingly contradictory commandments: regular ritual practice, and only performing ritual practices if your heart in committed to them. Not saying the Shema prayer every day is a transgression, but saying it if I don't mean it is also a transgression... so what do you do? This is why Jewish theologians heavily stress allowing young Jews to explore the lesser transgressions and such grey areas in religious law. The only real solution is to let people find that balance through experience with the faith.<br /> <br /> <br /> Onto part 2:<br /> <br /> This is extremely common, and if you write a Jewish character that hasn't done something like this, we can kinda tell your exposure to Jews is limited. Doubt is natural, teenagers are rebellious, so Jews tend to have a few transgressions under their belts.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-16T12:47:51-07:00","id":1452523,"title":"","content":"Honestly, I don't understand the issue. Is something lost? Does it diminish the reputation of the original culture in some way?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3523-spencer-from-the-hills","name":"Spencer from The Hills","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"546"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-16T14:12:36-07:00","id":1452533,"title":"","content":"This is a subject I've given some thought to recently while prepping my Alice Springs Mage campaign. Non indigenous history of Alice is only 100 years old and a third of the town are aboriginal. If I'm going to make a game with nodes, ley lines and sites of power I can't ignore Arrernte belief and custom. It wouldn't feel real.<br /> <br /> I've approached it by being as respectful as possible to the foundational mythology and acknowledge the significance of local landmarks. But at the same time I know that some people will regard the subject matter as too important to play with. That's something that I can't avoid if I want to play the game in the town we live in.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26865-old-dog","name":"Old Dog","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"27"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-16T14:38:19-07:00","id":1452538,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Spencer from The Hills</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1452173-cultural-appropriation-in-gaming?p=1452523#post1452523\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Is something lost?</div> </div> </div> </div>Yes:<br /> <br /> People lose a say in their own culture to outsiders, who are then free to treat that culture as a commodity without respect, compensation, or acknowledgement to the existing culture's actual people.<br /> <br /> People lose self-esteem and value in society when their identities are not theirs to define and control.<br /> <br /> Entire cultures are disappearing from the world because the people those cultures came from have been systematically denied agency in continuing their cultures on their own terms. Imagine how much poorer western culture would be if the Romans destroyed everything Greek, Egyptian, Phoenician, and Semitic from the world instead of (however flawed) recording things for history. The Epic of Gilgamesh was never available to be studied, the tombs and temples of Egypt flattened and destroyed, the Code of Hammurabi erased, the Torah stricken from memory.<br /> <br /> Cultural losses like that are happening, right now, and appropriation is one of the tools in colonialism that causes it.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Does it diminish the reputation of the original culture in some way? </div> </div> </div>Yes.<br /> <br /> Did you completely miss the part of how Christian appropriation of Jewish culture makes me feel like a prop? Do you not think that diminishes the reputation of Jews? Reducing us rhetorical points in political arguments?<br /> <br /> If you think Marjorie Taylor Greene and other right wing Americans comparing face masks to the treatment of Jews in the Holocaust is anti-Semitic, then you understand the flaws in appropriation.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":7}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-16T15:32:16-07:00","id":1452547,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1452173-cultural-appropriation-in-gaming?p=1452509#post1452509\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Just in general, it's frequently perceived as rude to ask one member of a minority to &amp;quot;answer&amp;quot; for the actions of another. As I said before, we're not a monolith. Judaism, more like an ethnic group than many religious groups, has no centralized leadership. There's no head rabbi dictating what is, and isn't appropriate Jewish behavior. The only religious authorities Jews recognize are: parents until their child is acknowledged as an adult by religious standards (the Bar Mitzvah ceremony), and the rabbi(s) an individual Jew chooses to follow (this is actually something that extends past religion in Jewish philosophy; Jews are expected to follow the advice of experts when they ask experts for advice, and thus are also told to be careful in picking their experts).<br /> <br /> So... while I think I get where you're coming from, please try to think of better ways to go about these sorts of questions (and this doesn't just apply to Jews).<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> So, I'll address two things here. First is my best attempt to relate Jewish theological philosophy to the situation. Second, I'll address this as, &amp;quot;HA, if you saw this character in a work, how would it make you feel?&amp;quot;<br /> <br /> The first one is going to get a bit lengthy, so I'll start with the short version: While Judaism would consider this a transgression, and thus it would require ritual cleansing of the body and the soul to avoid negative divine judgement, most Jewish philosophers would say that it's more important to value the commandments of our faith from a place of wisdom than one of ignorance. It's better to transgress and know what not keeping Kosher is like if that helps you appreciate Kashrut, than to follow the laws by rote and have no real attachment to them.<br /> <br /> In Jewish moral philosophy, there are only three transgressions that have no exceptions. That is, every other rule in Judaism comes with caveats, and exists in a hierarchy of levels of transgressions, mitigating factors, and so on. It's important to note that Judaism does not phrase these three transgressions as inherently ones that come with specific punishments at mortal hands (though we do that too), but rather these are the three sins that you should die before you commit: murder, sexual violence, and idolatry (not going to get into that one in detail, but this is its own very complicated subject).<br /> <br /> So, where does keeping Kosher fit into all this? Surprisingly low actually. As long as it's just between you and the Lord, eating something unkosher is ultimately a fairly low down sin. There is no case where, if you had to pick between two transgressions, eating something unkosher isn't the clear choice to make. Even when it wouldn't be clear, it's a coin-flip between two equally less important ones. Remember, this is the maximum punishment for breaking these laws (again, just for yourself): say a prayer and take a ritual bath. That's all you have to do before you can reclaim your position as a Jew-in-good-standing to participate in Jewish ritual life. You can see why the rabbis do not think harshly of exploration of transgressions on this level in younger Jews.<br /> <br /> Of course, things get more complicated when other people get involved. Taking your impressionable younger sibling to your pepperoni pizza party is not OK. Rabbis and other prominent Jews are held to a higher standard lest their actions led others astray by creating a sense that it is not a transgression (however minor of one). And so on.<br /> <br /> And, to come from the other part of this, Judaism has a long standing internal debate over two seemingly contradictory commandments: regular ritual practice, and only performing ritual practices if your heart in committed to them. Not saying the Shema prayer every day is a transgression, but saying it if I don't mean it is also a transgression... so what do you do? This is why Jewish theologians heavily stress allowing young Jews to explore the lesser transgressions and such grey areas in religious law. The only real solution is to let people find that balance through experience with the faith.<br /> <br /> <br /> Onto part 2:<br /> <br /> This is extremely common, and if you write a Jewish character that hasn't done something like this, we can kinda tell your exposure to Jews is limited. Doubt is natural, teenagers are rebellious, so Jews tend to have a few transgressions under their belts.</div> </div> </div> </div>Thank you. I like learning stuff and that perspective was really helpful. But it\u2019s not a fictional character. It\u2019s a real person. And I agree with you that comparing face masks to the treatment of Jews in the Holocaust is anti-Semitic.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"","name":"Guest","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/default/default_avatar_large.png","title":"","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-17T00:15:45-07:00","id":1452587,"title":"","content":"As an European, cultural appropriation is not really part of my cultural or political framework. A lot of cultures coexist in the society I live in, and it has been influenced by ideas, cuisines, and languages they brought. There's no such thing as a White way of doing things, or Eritrean way, or Lebanese, or Afghani, etc, etc. The roleplaying games written and produced here still has golems in them, some use mana as their mystical energy, and portray orcs (and other races) as violent and destructive forces in the realm, and not once did anyone in my group of players (some of which are immigrants) raise concerns about cultural appropriation or other cultural/societal misrepresentations in our games.<br /> <br /> Much less, we draw parallels between our roleplaying games and the perpetuation of imperialism and colonialism.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12103-vintervalpen","name":"Vintervalpen","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12103_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"83"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-17T01:15:54-07:00","id":1452589,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Vintervalpen</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1452173-cultural-appropriation-in-gaming?p=1452587#post1452587\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">As an European, cultural appropriation is not really part of my cultural or political framework.</div> </div> </div> </div>What you mean is, cultural appropriation is not part of your local political discourse. As a fellow European I can partly corroborate that. Here in Sweden it's a rare topic outside of Left wing political groups. Most people I know IRL would probably disregard the topic entirely, which is a problem of its own.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> A lot of cultures coexist in the society I live in, and it has been influenced by ideas, cuisines, and languages they brought. There's no such thing as a White way of doing things, or Eritrean way, or Lebanese, or Afghani, etc, etc. </div> </div> </div>A lot of modern cultural appropriation falls on &quot;exotic&quot; cultures that we don't have much direct contact with. For example, there is absolutely a Native American way of doing things that is different from our way of doing things, as well as a Japanese way of doing things, and we do create both Native American and Japanese inspired art and entertainment with little to no understanding of the significance of much of it. And if you're Swedish or Norwegian as your handle suggests, then you absolutely do have communities of people living somewhat separately and desperately trying to preserve what's left of their culture: The Sapmi.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The roleplaying games written and produced here still has golems in them, some use mana as their mystical energy, and portray orcs (and other races) as violent and destructive forces in the realm, and not once did anyone in my group of players (some of which are immigrants) raise concerns about cultural appropriation or other cultural/societal misrepresentations in our games.<br /> <br /> Much less, we draw parallels between our roleplaying games and the perpetuation of imperialism and colonialism. </div> </div> </div> That no one has raised concerns only means none of you <i>sees</i> it as a problem (or, at most, not a problem worth bringing up). It does not mean it <i>isn't</i> a problem.<br /> That some of your players are immigrants doesn't really mean anything unless they're from a culture that has &quot;inspired&quot; some of those games you mentioned, and it generally just sounds like &quot;some of my friends are black&quot; argument.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Writer for <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/410713/Bloodlines-The-Ageless\" target=\"_blank\">Bloodlines: The Ageless</a> and <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/491569/Fire--Revolution-Carthians\" target=\"_blank\">Fire &amp; Revolution: Carthians</a> on STV<br /> Some other stuff I've done: Ordo Dracul Mysteries: <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1138490-idea-coil-of-owls?p=1140522\" target=\"_blank\">Mystery of Smoke</a>, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1227848-mystery-of-%C5%BEiva-house-rule-revision\" target=\"_blank\">Revised Mystery of \u017diva</a> Mage The Awakening: <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/0B2LMDRtfCcpdZDU1dWJzT1dNeEE/view\" target=\"_blank\">Spell Quick Reference</a> (single page and landscape for computer screens)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11887-tessie","name":"Tessie","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11887_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4406"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-17T07:24:50-07:00","id":1452625,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Tessie</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1452173-cultural-appropriation-in-gaming?p=1452589#post1452589\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">What you mean is, cultural appropriation is not part of your local political discourse. As a fellow European I can partly corroborate that. Here in Sweden it's a rare topic outside of Left wing political groups. Most people I know IRL would probably disregard the topic entirely, which is a problem of its own.</div> </div> </div> </div>That is exactly what I meant. It's difficult for me to perceive this as a problem... since... well... no one I know experiences it as one. It feels particularly weird when people of privilege (usually white middle-class people) is telling me on behalf of specific minorities that do not even live on the same continent as me wants me to act or talk in a certain way. Sometimes, it's like they want to import drama. I might just not be very knowledgeable on the topic which makes me not see the specific issues at hand.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> A lot of modern cultural appropriation falls on &quot;exotic&quot; cultures that we don't have much direct contact with. For example, there is absolutely a Native American way of doing things that is different from our way of doing things, as well as a Japanese way of doing things, and we do create both Native American and Japanese inspired art and entertainment with little to no understanding of the significance of much of it. And if you're Swedish or Norwegian as your handle suggests, then you absolutely do have communities of people living somewhat separately and desperately trying to preserve what's left of their culture: The Sapmi. </div> </div> </div>As far as I know, the Sapmi people's identitarian and cultural struggles are not related to RPG's appropriating their culture. Again, not super knowledgeable on that specific topic.<br /> <br /> Well, yes, there is media and art that is inspired by other cultures. This has been true in many eras and in many countries. People create viking-inspired art, movies, books, etc, and the Nordic people trying to preserve the Norse culture are generally <i><b>ecstatic </b></i>about it even people don't get it right. They don't have to understand the significance of my roots to celebrate or emulate it. Anyway, I had a similar conversation a while back and it was pointed out to me that people coming from a history of oppression experience the same thing in a mostly negative manner, but I just wouldn't know that if someone hadn't told me.<br /> <br /> Personally, I think cultures, languages, and religions blending together is a beautiful thing.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> That no one has raised concerns only means none of you <i>sees</i> it as a problem (or, at most, not a problem worth bringing up). It does not mean it <i>isn't</i> a problem.<br /> That some of your players are immigrants doesn't really mean anything unless they're from a culture that has &quot;inspired&quot; some of those games you mentioned, and it generally just sounds like &quot;some of my friends are black&quot; argument. </div> </div> </div>You are probably right about that: none of us see mana or golems or orcs as a problem. RPGs to us are generally a form of escapism, and we're not generally trying to debate the finer points of real life politics in them -- I'm making my Daeva to explore personal horror and enjoy some political intrigue, not to get tangled up in the heritage of post-colonialism society. Which is probably also why most of the people you know IRL disregards the topic. From my conversations with other people I know, this is also the case in France, Holland, Great Britain, who were the nations that actually did the colonizing (i.e not America). That's more anecdotal than anything, but my point is kinda this feels like a very American politics issue to me.<br /> <br /> Again, I admit I am somewhat ignorant on this specific issue. I enjoy visiting other cultures, and I enjoy other cultures coming to visit mine. I think there should be more of an exchange, instead of less.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12103-vintervalpen","name":"Vintervalpen","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12103_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"83"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-17T09:21:50-07:00","id":1452642,"title":"","content":"I really wish I could find the article I read the other day about the wendigo. It explains a lot of the &quot;what's the big deal&quot; parts of this conversation very eloquently, and from the perspective of someone with actually cultural and scholarly authority.<br /> <br /> So the wendigo is part of a belief system. One that's still practiced today.<br /> <br /> People from outside that system take the wendigo and adapt it to their fictions, their novels and games. And it changes, like a game of telephone.<br /> <br /> Other people are exposed to those fictions and are inspired--not by the original concept, but by the fiction that was in turn inspired by the original concept--to include the wendigo in their own works. And it changes more.<br /> <br /> Soon, there is a substantial body of work--art, stories, online pseudo-mythology--that all uses the name &quot;wendigo&quot;. They share some common themes (giant evil deer-headed zombie/skeleton monster, etc).<br /> <br /> At this point, more people outside of the original culture are familiar with the deviant art/D&amp;D version of the wendigo than the original. What's more, *they don't even realize that what they consider a &quot;wendigo&quot; ISN'T the original*.<br /> <br /> And a cultural myth, a story that's a part of a living, breathing system of belief (and an extremely serious and important one, at that) is altered. Damaged in a way that it's people had no hand in and cannot repair.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":7}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26844-quixote","name":"Quixote","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"40"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-17T10:19:39-07:00","id":1452652,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Quixote</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1452173-cultural-appropriation-in-gaming?p=1452642#post1452642\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I really wish I could find the article I read the other day about the wendigo. It explains a lot of the &quot;what's the big deal&quot; parts of this conversation very eloquently, and from the perspective of someone with actually cultural and scholarly authority.<br /> <br /> So the wendigo is part of a belief system. One that's still practiced today.<br /> <br /> People from outside that system take the wendigo and adapt it to their fictions, their novels and games. And it changes, like a game of telephone.<br /> <br /> Other people are exposed to those fictions and are inspired--not by the original concept, but by the fiction that was in turn inspired by the original concept--to include the wendigo in their own works. And it changes more.<br /> <br /> Soon, there is a substantial body of work--art, stories, online pseudo-mythology--that all uses the name &quot;wendigo&quot;. They share some common themes (giant evil deer-headed zombie/skeleton monster, etc).<br /> <br /> At this point, more people outside of the original culture are familiar with the deviant art/D&amp;D version of the wendigo than the original. What's more, *they don't even realize that what they consider a &quot;wendigo&quot; ISN'T the original*.<br /> <br /> And a cultural myth, a story that's a part of a living, breathing system of belief (and an extremely serious and important one, at that) is altered. Damaged in a way that it's people had no hand in and cannot repair.</div> </div> </div> </div>Isn't that how culture and religion and systems of belief has always been changing and evolving, though, even before Christianity, and even outside the borders of European influence? Culture is always in a state of flux, always changing, that game of telephone tracing back basically till the start of human interaction? I always felt like embracing new thoughts and ideas and influences from people of backgrounds that differ from your own was an inherently good thing, that cultural exchange was an inherently good thing. I've been criticized for making a Caucasion character that had dreadlocks in a CoD game, and it just doesn't make sense to me.<br /> <br /> If you do find that article I would definitely be interested in reading it.<br /> <br /> EDIT:<br /> To clarify, I absolutely think there are times and places when cultures or religions were attacked in one way or another. That sort of oppression has hurt people, and it have made people lose connection with their heritage. I just see that as a different issue (and one that is definitely a lot easier to understand the ramifications of). Music, art, and entertainment being influenced by cultures different than your own has existed in all times and in all places. Bulgarian architecture was influenced by Byzantium architecture. Tang sculptures were inspired by the Greco-Buddhist art of Central Asia. I just don't see how we're ever going to reach a place where someone's deciding which cultures can be inspired by what other cultures and vice versa without a group of people objecting to it. It'd just be a very... I don't know... static world.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":12103,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12103-vintervalpen","user":"Vintervalpen","date":". 06-17-2021, 01:33 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12103-vintervalpen","name":"Vintervalpen","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12103_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"83"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-17T11:07:01-07:00","id":1452655,"title":"","content":"I believe very strongly in cultural sensitivity, but I also believe there\u2019s a limit.<br /> <br /> Case in point<br /> <br /> I\u2019m a white girl. Half WASP, half Italian. I also like pop culture stuff about the Mafia even though I\u2019m fully aware that it\u2019s only a small portion of my heritage (actually, technically none at all, since the Mafia is Sicilian and my heritage is Neapolitan) and much of it is grossly inaccurate. I like it even though my dad was falsely accused of being a Mafioso because a bunch of politicians thought any Italian in the bar and club business was mobbed up. And you know who taught me to love mob movies and the Sopranos? My dad.<br /> <br /> I\u2019ve spoken to <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar617_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"617\" data-vbnamecard=\"617\">Heavy Arms</a> about cultural appropriation and I trust his judgment on this sensitive subject, but I can\u2019t help thinking it wouldn\u2019t be the worst thing in the world to happen to the Navajo nation if someone used skinwalkers in an RPG, and it definitely shouldn\u2019t be illegal (First Amendment issues).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":25665,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/","user":"Guest","date":". 06-17-2021, 02:39 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"","name":"Guest","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/default/default_avatar_large.png","title":"","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-17T12:40:44-07:00","id":1452671,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Vintervalpen</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1452173-cultural-appropriation-in-gaming?p=1452652#post1452652\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Isn't that how culture and religion and systems of belief has always been changing and evolving, though, even before Christianity, and even outside the borders of European influence?...I always felt like embracing new thoughts and ideas and influences from people of backgrounds that differ from your own was an inherently good thing, that cultural exchange was an inherently good thing.</div> </div> </div> </div>Absolutely. And I agree; celebrating different cultures and the exchange between them is awesome.<br /> <br /> But specifically with the wendigo example, the Algonquin people have been the target of literal and cultural genocide. Their peoples were killed or forcibly removed from their homes, and their children were essentially brainwashed in an attempt to ensure that the next generation would have an outlook more to their oppressor's liking.<br /> So...I dunno. I feel like I owe it to them? Like, their people and their culture have been through enough, without people like me thoughtlessly tromping all over their traditions and stories, warping them into something unrecognizable and sending it out to a wide audience without any sort of nod to where those stories came from or how my take on them may differ.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Penelope</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1452173-cultural-appropriation-in-gaming?p=1452655#post1452655\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I can\u2019t help thinking it wouldn\u2019t be the worst thing in the world to happen to the Navajo nation if someone used skinwalkers in an RPG, and it definitely shouldn\u2019t be illegal (First Amendment issues).</div> </div> </div> </div>Not illegal, of course. But maybe insensitive. And, in the context of my above comments, I have come to understand how such things can and are sometimes viewed as outright attacks on the cultures involved.<br /> <br /> Granted, I think the main thread running through all of this is &quot;have good intentions, try your best, don't be a jerk&quot;, and that following such a creed is going to get us 99% of the way there. But sometimes ignorance without any sort of malice at all can still cause harm.<br /> <br /> As far as a skinwalker story...I would be willing to share the basic outline I've got so far in another thread, if anyone would like to take a look and see if I've done okay.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26844-quixote","name":"Quixote","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"40"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-17T12:48:03-07:00","id":1452674,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Vintervalpen</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1452173-cultural-appropriation-in-gaming?p=1452652#post1452652\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">To clarify, I absolutely think there are times and places when cultures or religions were attacked in one way or another.</div> </div> </div> </div>The problem is that time has been &quot;the last 10,000 years, continuing to this day.&quot; That the place isn't where you physically are doesn't mean it's not happening, as it has always happened.<br /> <br /> If you acknowledge that minority groups get attacked, what's so hard about listening to us when we're saying something is a form of attacking us just because you don't personally see it happening?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> That sort of oppression has hurt people, and it have made people lose connection with their heritage. </div> </div> </div>Please stop using the past tense. This is happening around the world, right now. It's not something that only happened in history. You, whether you see it or not, are a beneficiary of colonialism. You are gaining at the expense and harm of others even if your country wasn't one of the big colonial powers. Other people are suffering for your life to be better. Allowing the distance between you and them to shield this from your cultural framework only helps perpetuate the harm being done.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I just see that as a different issue </div> </div> </div>That's easy to say when nobody in your family has had their creative works stolen from them for others to profit from. Everything is pretty when you can't see the pile of bodies trampled on to make that beautiful artistic work.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I just don't see how we're ever going to reach a place where someone's deciding which cultures can be inspired by what other cultures and vice versa without a group of people objecting to it. It'd just be a very... I don't know... static world. </div> </div> </div>This isn't really a good faith response to the issue of cultural appropriation.<br /> <br /> Let's look at a fairly simple and direct comparison: Rock &amp; roll vs. hip hop.<br /> <br /> Rock &amp; roll and hip hop are both musical genres that were created by black American subculture.<br /> <br /> Rock &amp; roll was heavily appropriated. First by white American culture, and then by white culture in general, and by global culture. Extremely few of the creators of rock &amp; roll got rich, while a lot of white people got rich off them. And rock &amp; roll didn't get dynamic for it, it got static. For the first 30 years or so of its popularity, with the originators pushed out, the appropriators were just remixing the same material over and over for pop audiences. When you think of the revival of rock &amp; roll's creativity in the 70s and 80s, it wasn't because global exposure to the music led to all sorts of innovations... it's because white people starting adding black and latin innovations to their work. The only reason we have hard rock and heavy metal, is because white people starting appropriating the blues to spice up how dull rock &amp; roll had become in their hands.<br /> <br /> The hip hop and rap artists grew up with that history. So they fought it. They pushed to keep control over their work, push against appropriation, and to make sure that other artists that wanted in &quot;earned&quot; it instead of took it. Are there non-black hip hop groups? Of course there are. But it never stops being a black cultural expression that other people are knowingly engaging in. The pioneers of it are acknowledged and elevated instead of shoved aside for more mainstream faces. Black people haven't been shut out of the economic and social success their creativity generates. Hip hop didn't stagnate into some static art form. It's constantly evolved, and frequently from positive cultural exchanges rather than apropriative ones.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":7}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-17T12:50:27-07:00","id":1452675,"title":"","content":"Can I read your story?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"","name":"Guest","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/default/default_avatar_large.png","title":"","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-17T12:55:13-07:00","id":1452678,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1452173-cultural-appropriation-in-gaming?p=1452674#post1452674\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> The problem is that time has been &amp;quot;the last 10,000 years, continuing to this day.&amp;quot; That the place isn't where you physically are doesn't mean it's not happening, as it has always happened.<br /> <br /> If you acknowledge that minority groups get attacked, what's so hard about listening to us when we're saying something is a form of attacking us just because you don't personally see it happening?<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> Please stop using the past tense. This is happening around the world, right now. It's not something that only happened in history. You, whether you see it or not, are a beneficiary of colonialism. You are gaining at the expense and harm of others even if your country wasn't one of the big colonial powers. Other people are suffering for your life to be better. Allowing the distance between you and them to shield this from your cultural framework only helps perpetuate the harm being done.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> That's easy to say when nobody in your family has had their creative works stolen from them for others to profit from. Everything is pretty when you can't see the pile of bodies trampled on to make that beautiful artistic work.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> This isn't really a good faith response to the issue of cultural appropriation.<br /> <br /> Let's look at a fairly simple and direct comparison: Rock &amp; roll vs. hip hop.<br /> <br /> Rock &amp; roll and hip hop are both musical genres that were created by black American subculture.<br /> <br /> Rock &amp; roll was heavily appropriated. First by white American culture, and then by white culture in general, and by global culture. Extremely few of the creators of rock &amp; roll got rich, while a lot of white people got rich off them. And rock &amp; roll didn't get dynamic for it, it got static. For the first 30 years or so of its popularity, with the originators pushed out, the appropriators were just remixing the same material over and over for pop audiences. When you think of the revival of rock &amp; roll's creativity in the 70s and 80s, it wasn't because global exposure to the music led to all sorts of innovations... it's because white people starting adding black and latin innovations to their work. The only reason we have hard rock and heavy metal, is because white people starting appropriating the blues to spice up how dull rock &amp; roll had become in their hands.<br /> <br /> The hip hop and rap artists grew up with that history. So they fought it. They pushed to keep control over their work, push against appropriation, and to make sure that other artists that wanted in &amp;quot;earned&amp;quot; it instead of took it. Are there non-black hip hop groups? Of course there are. But it never stops being a black cultural expression that other people are knowingly engaging in. The pioneers of it are acknowledged and elevated instead of shoved aside for more mainstream faces. Black people haven't been shut out of the economic and social success their creativity generates. Hip hop didn't stagnate into some static art form. It's constantly evolved, and frequently from positive cultural exchanges rather than apropriative ones.</div> </div> </div> </div>This is a really good analysis. Thank you.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"","name":"Guest","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/default/default_avatar_large.png","title":"","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-17T13:30:31-07:00","id":1452685,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1452173-cultural-appropriation-in-gaming?p=1452674#post1452674\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Let's look at a fairly simple and direct comparison: Rock &amp; roll vs. hip hop.<br /> <br /> Rock &amp; roll and hip hop are both musical genres that were created by black American subculture.<br /> <br /> Rock &amp; roll was heavily appropriated. First by white American culture, and then by white culture in general, and by global culture. Extremely few of the creators of rock &amp; roll got rich, while a lot of white people got rich off them. And rock &amp; roll didn't get dynamic for it, it got static. For the first 30 years or so of its popularity, with the originators pushed out, the appropriators were just remixing the same material over and over for pop audiences. When you think of the revival of rock &amp; roll's creativity in the 70s and 80s, it wasn't because global exposure to the music led to all sorts of innovations... it's because white people starting adding black and latin innovations to their work. The only reason we have hard rock and heavy metal, is because white people starting appropriating the blues to spice up how dull rock &amp; roll had become in their hands.<br /> <br /> The hip hop and rap artists grew up with that history. So they fought it. They pushed to keep control over their work, push against appropriation, and to make sure that other artists that wanted in &quot;earned&quot; it instead of took it. Are there non-black hip hop groups? Of course there are. But it never stops being a black cultural expression that other people are knowingly engaging in. The pioneers of it are acknowledged and elevated instead of shoved aside for more mainstream faces. Black people haven't been shut out of the economic and social success their creativity generates. Hip hop didn't stagnate into some static art form. It's constantly evolved, and frequently from positive cultural exchanges rather than apropriative ones.</div> </div> </div> </div>Do you think white people would have bought so many records at the time, if they weren't made by other white people?<br /> <br /> Suppose you have one community of 10,000 who have some sort of cultural product. And suppose that somebody from a more powerful community imitates it, popularising it to 90,000 people. Only the 10% who started it have the real thing, but what's stopping them from keeping it and developing it amongst themselves, as they did before it was copied?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3523-spencer-from-the-hills","name":"Spencer from The Hills","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"546"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-17T13:44:16-07:00","id":1452688,"title":"","content":"I started typing this reply some hours ago. Then dinner and stuff happened and it seems the discussion went on. Anyway.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Vintervalpen</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1452173-cultural-appropriation-in-gaming?p=1452625#post1452625\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">That is exactly what I meant. It's difficult for me to perceive this as a problem... since... well... no one I know experiences it as one. It feels particularly weird when people of privilege (usually white middle-class people) is telling me on behalf of specific minorities that do not even live on the same continent as me wants me to act or talk in a certain way. Sometimes, it's like they want to import drama. I might just not be very knowledgeable on the topic which makes me not see the specific issues at hand.</div> </div> </div> </div>Minorities tend to be in minority so they're often not heard except where they make themselves heard. IRL that tends to be in places or groups that they already have extensive contact with. Hence, if you're going to hear about it in person, it's probably going to be from the majority group.<br /> That said, it's absolutely reasonable to be wary of what people say about groups they do not belong to. As such, I probably wouldn't take myself at face value without further corroboration if I were in your shoes, but Heavy Arms has made some really good posts in this thread as well as representing a group they actually belong to.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> As far as I know, the Sapmi people's identitarian and cultural struggles are not related to RPG's appropriating their culture. Again, not super knowledgeable on that specific topic. </div> </div> </div>You're right. It's a bit off topic to this thread, but it was a counter example to how I interpreted your post as claiming or implying that minority groups are largely integrated to a shared culture when in actuality they often have a lot of cultural heritage we just don't see or recognise for what it is.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Well, yes, there is media and art that is inspired by other cultures. This has been true in many eras and in many countries. People create viking-inspired art, movies, books, etc, and the Nordic people trying to preserve the Norse culture are generally <i><b>ecstatic </b></i>about it even people don't get it right. They don't have to understand the significance of my roots to celebrate or emulate it. Anyway, I had a similar conversation a while back and it was pointed out to me that people coming from a history of oppression experience the same thing in a mostly negative manner, but I just wouldn't know that if someone hadn't told me. </div> </div> </div>I do like efforts to preserve Norse history and even efforts to revive some of them, but the truth is our Norse history is just history at this point. That has not been our way of life for almost a thousand years. Cultural appropriation often targets things with a long history, but they're still culturally alive. Stories, beliefs, arts, symbols, etc, that people still practice in some way or another.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> You are probably right about that: none of us see mana or golems or orcs as a problem. RPGs to us are generally a form of escapism, and we're not generally trying to debate the finer points of real life politics in them -- I'm making my Daeva to explore personal horror and enjoy some political intrigue, not to get tangled up in the heritage of post-colonialism society. Which is probably also why most of the people you know IRL disregards the topic. From my conversations with other people I know, this is also the case in France, Holland, Great Britain, who were the nations that actually did the colonizing (i.e not America). That's more anecdotal than anything, but my point is kinda this feels like a very American politics issue to me. </div> </div> </div>Firstly, no one has claimed you should explore any of those concepts in your games. This thread is about how you approach RPGs (specifically how to go about making content for your own games). RPGs, like most entertainment, is mainly a form of escapism. I wouldn't have fun in such a game either.<br /> But when I design content, or is just using premade content in my games, I just make sure I've research stuff that has a historical or cultural basis and try to not misrepresent it. It's just what I do to feel good about my content and I don't really discuss any of it with my players. It's effort, but it's effort I would've done anyway really because I think such research is generally also good for the game I'm running regardless of cultural contexts.<br /> <br /> Secondly, the damage is mostly already done by the publishers of those books. None of us are responsible for that. We're only responsible for the way we handle that content and the content we create ourselves.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Again, I admit I am somewhat ignorant on this specific issue. I enjoy visiting other cultures, and I enjoy other cultures coming to visit mine. I think there should be more of an exchange, instead of less. </div> </div> </div>That's actually the crux of the matter. Cultural appropriation is not an exchange.<br /> It can be likened to internet piracy: Copying things without their owners' permission. Things are shared without respect of their actual owners' say in the matter. However, the damages aren't loss of sales (although, depending on the product in question, piracy can actually lead to increased sales, but that's a very different and very complex topic) but rather loss of creative control. Like the golem or the wendigo examples described above, through careless handling and willful ignorance, the popcultural machine has stripped away a lot of the original identities and spat out two new things that have replaced the originals for most people.<br /> <br /> But actual cultural exchange, that's something I think everyone here finds positive.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Writer for <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/410713/Bloodlines-The-Ageless\" target=\"_blank\">Bloodlines: The Ageless</a> and <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/491569/Fire--Revolution-Carthians\" target=\"_blank\">Fire &amp; Revolution: Carthians</a> on STV<br /> Some other stuff I've done: Ordo Dracul Mysteries: <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1138490-idea-coil-of-owls?p=1140522\" target=\"_blank\">Mystery of Smoke</a>, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1227848-mystery-of-%C5%BEiva-house-rule-revision\" target=\"_blank\">Revised Mystery of \u017diva</a> Mage The Awakening: <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/0B2LMDRtfCcpdZDU1dWJzT1dNeEE/view\" target=\"_blank\">Spell Quick Reference</a> (single page and landscape for computer screens)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11887-tessie","name":"Tessie","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11887_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4406"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-17T13:44:40-07:00","id":1452689,"title":"","content":"So, because I'm apparently feeling a bit chatty, and maybe a tad cynically ironic...<br /> <br /> I want to explain what a golem really is. When I say golem, everyone thinks about magical constructs that have a habit of rebelling against their creators. Those aren't really golems. They're called golems in reference to what a true golem is.<br /> <br /> We, as in humanity, are golems. Or at least that's how we start. A golem is a person of ignorance. Someone that doesn't even understand that they don't know. In Jewish mysticism humanity is troubled by various failings that keep us from spiritual enlightenment. Being a golem is the first. You cannot seek enlightenment without knowing you lack it. We all have to have a moment of inspiration; an experience that makes us wonder in awe of the vastness of the universe around us, and sparks in us a desire to understand it. In the moment we cease to be golems, our ignorance is shattered and replaced by a drive to start down a path towards a higher state of being.<br /> <br /> In Jewish mysticism, it's believed that all of the failings of humanity are told in the story of Eden (lots of different ways to count them and differentiate them abound). If overcoming that first ignorance is in the story of Eden... it comes first. And the first failing of Adam is that unlike all other life in Eden, he was not simply created once. At first, he was shaped into a lump of dirt/clay. As such, he existed as a golem, a being unknowing even of itself. Only with the first divine breath does Adam get created a second time, as a knowing human, who then begins the process of understanding his world (the naming of all the things in Eden).<br /> <br /> Of course, the connection to the golem-as-creation of man should be more obvious now. When a Jewish mystic creates a golem, they are copying the creation of Adam. They're called golems because they're stuck in that state: created once, but not being granted the divine breath that would free them of their limited state. And why they don't get that second creation is down to the different golem stories (there are many) and what the storyteller is trying to convey. Golem stories in this sense are cautionary tales about the other failings along the path to spiritual enlightenment. When you have progressed far enough, you have the knowledge to create ignorance, and deny awe, and that power is like all power; dangerous. Humans are not meant to be stuck in that state, and they will push against such wrongful imprisonment.<br /> <br /> So, when I look at a game like Promethean... of course I'm comfortable with how golems are used. Promethean is a game all about what the true point of golem stories are supposed to be about. It's a mystic journey to become a full and true human... to cease to be a golem... it's about as good of a secularized version of things as I can imagine.<br /> <br /> But when I look at a monster manual with a golem reduced to a clay monster (or worse golems of other materials), not only is it disrespectful to my culture and traditions... I'm looking at some bland pointless thing taking the name of something deep and spiritual. Golem stories don't exist to have a monster to fight, they exist to keep us humble on our path towards being better people. They exist to remind us of where we started and no lose sight of that. Calling a statblock that gets bonuses because it's made of clay, and can be killed if you target it's forehead just right a golem is denying yourself a far more fulfilling story.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":10}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-17T13:55:17-07:00","id":1452690,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Spencer from The Hills</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1452173-cultural-appropriation-in-gaming?p=1452685#post1452685\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Do you think white people would have bought so many records at the time, if they weren't made by other white people?</div> </div> </div> </div>Yes. White people bought plenty of black music. But they bought it from other white people who didn't compensate the black artists fairly. The number of black artists that died poor despite selling lots of tickets and records belies any idea that racism meant white people were actually uncomfortable with black people as entertainers.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Only the 10% who started it have the real thing, but what's stopping them from keeping it and developing it amongst themselves, as they did before it was copied? </div> </div> </div>Not having the time to do so because they need to get paying work to survive instead of being fairly elevated by their contributions to society so they can keep doing that. The next generations will come up with their own things in their spare time, but the old cultural elements will be lost within the community as the desire to preserve it for its own sake is hard to keep up without significant wealth behind it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-17T14:05:15-07:00","id":1452691,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Tessie</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1452173-cultural-appropriation-in-gaming?p=1452688#post1452688\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">That's actually the crux of the matter. Cultural appropriation is not an exchange.<br /> It can be likened to internet piracy: Copying things without their owners' permission. Things are shared without respect of their actual owners' say in the matter. However, the damages aren't loss of sales (although, depending on the product in question, piracy can actually lead to increased sales, but that's a very different and very complex topic) but rather loss of creative control. Like the golem or the wendigo examples described above, through careless handling and willful ignorance, the popcultural machine has stripped away a lot of the original identities and spat out two new things that have replaced the originals for most people.<br /> <br /> But actual cultural exchange, that's something I think everyone here finds positive.</div> </div> </div> </div>I liked this. It's made me think a little bit differently about this.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12103-vintervalpen","name":"Vintervalpen","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12103_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"83"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-17T17:15:20-07:00","id":1452719,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Penelope</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1452173-cultural-appropriation-in-gaming?p=1452675#post1452675\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Can I read your story?</div> </div> </div> </div>I started a new thread titled &quot;Uintah Basin ghost story.&quot; The bones of it are there.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26844-quixote","name":"Quixote","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"40"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-18T04:43:23-07:00","id":1452752,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1452173-cultural-appropriation-in-gaming?p=1452689#post1452689\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So, because I'm apparently feeling a bit chatty, and maybe a tad cynically ironic...<br /> <br /> I want to explain what a golem really is. When I say golem, everyone thinks about magical constructs that have a habit of rebelling against their creators. Those aren't really golems. They're called golems in reference to what a true golem is.<br /> <br /> We, as in humanity, are golems. Or at least that's how we start. A golem is a person of ignorance. Someone that doesn't even understand that they don't know. In Jewish mysticism humanity is troubled by various failings that keep us from spiritual enlightenment. Being a golem is the first. You cannot seek enlightenment without knowing you lack it. We all have to have a moment of inspiration; an experience that makes us wonder in awe of the vastness of the universe around us, and sparks in us a desire to understand it. In the moment we cease to be golems, our ignorance is shattered and replaced by a drive to start down a path towards a higher state of being.<br /> <br /> In Jewish mysticism, it's believed that all of the failings of humanity are told in the story of Eden (lots of different ways to count them and differentiate them abound). If overcoming that first ignorance is in the story of Eden... it comes first. And the first failing of Adam is that unlike all other life in Eden, he was not simply created once. At first, he was shaped into a lump of dirt/clay. As such, he existed as a golem, a being unknowing even of itself. Only with the first divine breath does Adam get created a second time, as a knowing human, who then begins the process of understanding his world (the naming of all the things in Eden).<br /> <br /> Of course, the connection to the golem-as-creation of man should be more obvious now. When a Jewish mystic creates a golem, they are copying the creation of Adam. They're called golems because they're stuck in that state: created once, but not being granted the divine breath that would free them of their limited state. And why they don't get that second creation is down to the different golem stories (there are many) and what the storyteller is trying to convey. Golem stories in this sense are cautionary tales about the other failings along the path to spiritual enlightenment. When you have progressed far enough, you have the knowledge to create ignorance, and deny awe, and that power is like all power; dangerous. Humans are not meant to be stuck in that state, and they will push against such wrongful imprisonment.<br /> <br /> So, when I look at a game like Promethean... of course I'm comfortable with how golems are used. Promethean is a game all about what the true point of golem stories are supposed to be about. It's a mystic journey to become a full and true human... to cease to be a golem... it's about as good of a secularized version of things as I can imagine.<br /> <br /> But when I look at a monster manual with a golem reduced to a clay monster (or worse golems of other materials), not only is it disrespectful to my culture and traditions... I'm looking at some bland pointless thing taking the name of something deep and spiritual. Golem stories don't exist to have a monster to fight, they exist to keep us humble on our path towards being better people. They exist to remind us of where we started and no lose sight of that. Calling a statblock that gets bonuses because it's made of clay, and can be killed if you target it's forehead just right a golem is denying yourself a far more fulfilling story.<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>Apart from the legend of the Golem of Prague i did not knew anything else existed about Golem s &quot;legend&quot;.<br /> Now i understand your discomfort, it s akin to open a monster manual and seeing &quot;evil human fetus&quot; showed as a mindless flesh eating enemy (at least for me, i would feel in a similar way i think).<br /> Thanks for sharing it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24346-neos01","name":"Neos01","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24346_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1021"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-18T05:17:24-07:00","id":1452759,"title":"","content":"I found this whole thread very enlightening. Now I think I understand better what cultural appropriation really is.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"","name":"Guest","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/default/default_avatar_large.png","title":"","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-19T01:36:48-07:00","id":1452871,"title":"","content":"Ditto, while I knew the story and presumed there was an allegorical meaning in the sense Heavy Arms mentioned, I wasn't aware it was a general concept used.<br /> <br /> A bit further from CofD, but it gives a bit of insight to something in Magic the Gathering; early sets had a pretty standard D&amp;D style of golem, but later ones made it clear golems could have personality and were basically a fantasy version of a sci-fi robotic AI, whereas things that were merely clever machines were usually typed Construct.<br /> <br /> One of the first turning points was in the Weatherlight Saga, which features a silver golem named Karn who is portrayed as a fully fleshed-out character (by the standards of card games about wizards summoning beasties to throw at an opponent); created by one of the greatest artificers, a sworn pacifist after an action of his harmed someone, and eventually becomes an accomplished artificer and planeswalker in his own right. Popular character to this day.<br /> <br /> And one of the creators of the Weatherlight Saga storyline is Magic's current lead designer, Mark Rosewater, who is Jewish. And now I wonder how much one might have to do with the other, re: treating a golem as a person as opposed to just an animated statue monster.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2334-aliasi","name":"Aliasi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2334_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"197"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-19T04:56:42-07:00","id":1452886,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aliasi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1452173-cultural-appropriation-in-gaming?p=1452871#post1452871\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">A bit further from CofD, but it gives a bit of insight to something in Magic the Gathering; early sets had a pretty standard D&amp;D style of golem, but later ones made it clear golems could have personality and were basically a fantasy version of a sci-fi robotic AI, whereas things that were merely clever machines were usually typed Construct.</div> </div> </div> </div>It's kinda funny how these things go in weird full circles. The Golem of Prague was (stated I'm not sure if ever recorded) to be one of Capek's inspirations for RUR, which popularized the word robot, but &quot;robot&quot; drifted away from artificial human towards high-tech machines, then sci-fi AIs come into recreate the artificial human aspect in a mechanical form, and we come back to the original exploration of humanity through the lens of a non-human created by humans.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-19T08:21:23-07:00","id":1452898,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aliasi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1452173-cultural-appropriation-in-gaming?p=1452871#post1452871\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Ditto, while I knew the story and presumed there was an allegorical meaning in the sense Heavy Arms mentioned, I wasn't aware it was a general concept used.<br /> <br /> A bit further from CofD, but it gives a bit of insight to something in Magic the Gathering; early sets had a pretty standard D&amp;D style of golem, but later ones made it clear golems could have personality and were basically a fantasy version of a sci-fi robotic AI, whereas things that were merely clever machines were usually typed Construct.<br /> <br /> One of the first turning points was in the Weatherlight Saga, which features a silver golem named Karn who is portrayed as a fully fleshed-out character (by the standards of card games about wizards summoning beasties to throw at an opponent); created by one of the greatest artificers, a sworn pacifist after an action of his harmed someone, and eventually becomes an accomplished artificer and planeswalker in his own right. Popular character to this day.<br /> <br /> And one of the creators of the Weatherlight Saga storyline is Magic's current lead designer, Mark Rosewater, who is Jewish. And now I wonder how much one might have to do with the other, re: treating a golem as a person as opposed to just an animated statue monster.<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>Fascinating!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"","name":"Guest","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/default/default_avatar_large.png","title":"","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-23T04:38:28-07:00","id":1453260,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1452173-cultural-appropriation-in-gaming?p=1452247#post1452247\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">D'jinn are definitely not part of my culture.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm assuming you are an American Ashkenazi, then?<br /> <br /> Not all of us are a single &quot;culture&quot;. <br /> When the Romans stole our homeland and cast us away, we went every which way - not only to Europe. <br /> <br /> I'm mixed, I have one side that came from Europe and another that came from an Arabic country.<br /> I had recent ancestors called Abdullah and Fatima. When I was a kid, running around the adults in family gatherings, they spoke Arabic with each other. When you were eating your kneidalach and your gefilte fish, I was having tabit and baklawa. <br /> <br /> You may dismiss Djinn as not part of your culture, but they are part of <i>my</i> culture. My grandfather told me stories about Djinn when I was a kid.<br /> <br /> We have many cultures.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1807-serpent-axis","name":"Serpent Axis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1807_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"135"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-23T12:13:33-07:00","id":1453305,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Serpent Axis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1452173-cultural-appropriation-in-gaming?p=1453260#post1453260\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm assuming you are an American Ashkenazi, then?</div> </div> </div> </div>Uh, yes? I might not have talked about that specifically on this thread, but I've mentioned this many times over the years here. Though I have both Sephardi and Mizrahi members of my family.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Not all of us are a single &quot;culture&quot; </div> </div> </div>Yes, hence my repeated use of things to indicate my own personal view, and multiple stresses to not view cultures as monoliths.<br /> .<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> When the Romans stole our homeland and cast us away, we went every which way - not only to Europe. </div> </div> </div>Do you... really think I don't know about this?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> When you were eating your kneidalach and your gefilte fish, I was having tabit and baklawa. </div> </div> </div>Um, is there a specific point to stereotyping Ashkenazim like this? Yeah, that is what my mother's side of the family treated as traditional food, but my father's side of the family is Ukrainian, which still gets lumped into Ashkenaz, but they didn't speak Yiddish, and ate very different foods (because Ukrainian food ain't German food). Germany and Ukraine might not be as distant as your sides of your families, but I have two rather distinct cultural influences on my life from that perspective.<br /> <br /> The only reason I haven't been chowing down on baklawa since I was too young to remember is that me and certain nuts don't get along. So when extended family get togethers happen, I angle for some malabi from one of my cousins (also hers is just fantastic and I can't find anything in the US in stores that deserves the name, but I'm biased).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> You may dismiss Djinn as not part of your culture, but they are part of <i>my</i> culture. </div> </div> </div>OK, you do realize I don't claim them as part of my culture because (a) they're not and (b) so that people that do consider them part of their culture can speak to how to use them respectfully for themselves?<br /> <br /> My whole point is that I don't want to speak for you, as someone with a deep connection to those stories, on how people should approach them. I would love it if you'd be up for sharing your thoughts on d'jinn in pop-culture for the thread as someone that has them as part of their culture. Despite us both being Jews, those aren't my stories to tell. If they're your stories to tell awesome. As you can see, a lot of people besides me here would appreciate perspectives they aren't exposed to.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":617,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","user":"Heavy Arms","date":". 06-23-2021, 03:15 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-23T13:15:58-07:00","id":1453307,"title":"","content":"Yeah, wow. Definitely looks like some miscommunication there.<br /> From what I've read, Heavy Arms's tone has been nothing but tentative and respectful. They weren't &quot;dismissing&quot; anything; they just weren't going to speak on a topic with which they have no personal experience or claim.<br /> <br /> And I agree. I would greatly appreciate any insight you have on the djinn and how they are represented in popular culture/media.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26844-quixote","name":"Quixote","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"40"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-23T16:04:16-07:00","id":1453316,"title":"","content":"I\u2019d also love to hear about it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"","name":"Guest","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/default/default_avatar_large.png","title":"","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-09-08T05:26:40-07:00","id":1459283,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1452173-cultural-appropriation-in-gaming?p=1453305#post1453305\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">(clipped post)</div> </div> </div> </div>Sorry for the absence. Both parents caught coronoa and I had a very scary, unfun time.<br /> My apologies for my prickly manner. It was a reactionary, Pavlovian response. I thought that by saying that Jinn aren't part of your heritage, you were basically saying that Middle-Eastern culture in general isn't part of Jewish heritage. I jumped a few conclusions there, and then took a few more leaps and a trip to Neptune, I acknowledge. I should not have assumed so much. <br /> <br /> I've faced some prejudice and outright racism in my life, not the least of which came from Ashkenazi peers. <br /> Like you, I have two halves, but despite that I still face <i>looks</i> and attitude. One of my earliest recollections is of a peer telling me how &quot;we're all brothers and sisters...&quot; and then stressed how I wasn't included in that. <br /> <br /> In fact, my most recent experience was when I was waiting in a line and was purposely passed over in favor of blond, blue eyed man (who even pointed out that I was there first, on my behalf, but to no avail). This kind of thing isn't very common nowadays, at least in my own personal life now. <br /> <br /> Anyway.<br /> <br /> As for the Jinn, I don't have profound spiritual or intellectual analyses like the ones you had for the golem. <br /> <br /> The one that I most liked was a tale about a kind of Jinn that I think in English is called <i>ghoul</i> (which I recall was called a &quot;Roola&quot; or &quot;Oola&quot;, but since I only ever heard that word <i>spoken</i> I don't know how it's written in any language). They looked like women, were more like witches, but dwelt in caves and much like Sirens, would lure you inside in order to devour you. <br /> <br /> Anyway, I was told a story about a hunter who lost his son to one of those things and went on a quest for vengeance against them. <br /> I don't remember the story perfectly (last time I heard it was maybe 20 years ago), so I'll avoid transcribing it. I do remember, when I went on a trip to our southern desert region, seeing those caves and canyons where these demonic witches supposedly laired and thinking about them.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1807-serpent-axis","name":"Serpent Axis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1807_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"135"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-09-08T13:32:50-07:00","id":1459336,"title":"","content":"Thank you for the post, and I hope that your parents are recovering to good health.<br /> <br /> I completely understand how the stresses of recent issues can dredge up more ugliness, and I'm glad you felt open to talk about your experiences. While I have been fortunate to look &quot;normal,&quot; I've seen the pain in my own family caused by prejudice (both from within and from outside) aimed at those that don't. This week especially reminds me of how my brother-in-law basically didn't see most of his family for years after 9/11 because they would usually gather for the High Holidays and the &quot;random stops&quot; at the airport got so bad he almost spent Rosh Ha'Shanah in one. My mind isn't a rest wondering how many Americans reflecting on twenty years later are reflecting on stories like that too.<br /> <br /> I'm sorry that I let defensiveness over the letter of my words get the better of the compassion I should have shown to the pain I should have recognized.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-09-09T10:10:29-07:00","id":1459448,"title":"","content":"I've been struggling with something I feel that it is appropriation, but I'm still learning to put into words.<br /> <br /> Since always, the way people outside South America think about Brazil just as Rio de Janeiro and jungle nags at me. I imagine things aren't better in a lot of other South American countries that don't get even that as recognition for their existence, but I can't talk for them.<br /> <br /> But the way White Wolf treats the Amazon forest worsened this in several ways. The Amazon isn't just present in the game, it is a big part of it. It is mentioned and talked about, and there is a war happening there.<br /> <br /> An European war. With eventual help of jungle dwellers.<br /> <br /> No mention of the people and cultures that live here. Barely a mention to cities, and then it is Rio de Janeiro, at most S\u00e3o Paulo. Sometimes mention to old Pre-Colombian ruins instead of their living descendants or the many people that have nothing to do with them.<br /> <br /> This feels wrong in many levels. Half a billion people live in the continent, Brazil alone is bigger than the contiguous US, we have 17 cities with more than a million inhabitants against 14 in the US, and just as many different landscapes if not more. And yet, here we are. The mighty Fera of the North came to save our forest and we're a footnote in the story...","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-09-09T10:56:27-07:00","id":1459451,"title":"","content":"I feel that the treatment of South America in the WoD tends to fall more into an unfortunate mix of environmentalism and White Man's Burden thinking, rather than being entirely appropriation. It's sort of the opposite problem where it's obvious the writers didn't really know much about... any level of detail, and decided to ignore the people and focus on foreigners coming in to deal with larger scope issues; rather than try to write South American stuff that's actually meant to represent the local cultures, topics of importance, and so on.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-09-09T14:08:01-07:00","id":1459469,"title":"","content":"And even the fact that such issues would already have a lot of local hands on them, and they do.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-09-11T08:59:17-07:00","id":1459663,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1452173-cultural-appropriation-in-gaming?p=1459451#post1459451\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I feel that the treatment of South America in the WoD tends to fall more into an unfortunate mix of environmentalism and White Man's Burden thinking, rather than being entirely appropriation. It's sort of the opposite problem where it's obvious the writers didn't really know much about... any level of detail, and decided to ignore the people and focus on foreigners coming in to deal with larger scope issues; rather than try to write South American stuff that's actually meant to represent the local cultures, topics of importance, and so on.</div> </div> </div> </div>At least it gets treated in a more complex way than here in Europe... I can tell you that there is outright disdain in France to everything down the Rio Grande (and when I mean disdain, I don't mean just stereotype that there is only narcos there, but really more the idea that &quot;those peoples&quot; have absolutely nothing of interest to teach us somehow, which i find disgusting), and it often shows in our creations... Even if younger creators/studios are starting to get away from it, thankfully.<br /> <br /> Regarding cultural appropriation, I think the main difference between it and culture heritage between cultures is often that it's a majority group taking something which used to be (and is often still) depicted as something dirty or &quot;primitive&quot; etc. (like with dreadlocks for instance or African braids worn by Europeans or European-descended peoples). Note that the reverse isn't true (except in the mind of jypocrites/delusional peoples I guess) : Afro-descendants or Africans straightening their hairs is not them stealing or appropriating from the Europeans, but clearly a<b>n attempt to conform to the beauty standards</b> of said Europeans. So the underlying reason is fundamentaly different and that's what is separating cultural appropriation from normal mixes and matches of cultures and cultural elements.<br /> <br /> And sometimes, even supposedly owned cultural elements can be said to be badly displayed (and in some case, I consider that said ownership is somewhat in bad faith). For instance, I really don't like the usual depiction of the Greek Gods (or any Pantheon from ancient Europe from before Abrahamic faith washed over it) in pop-culture because I'm a Neo-Pagan. But I understand that I can't do anything about it because the wide family of Neo-Paganism is a very small one and it's also corrupted by Far-Right extremists anyway who give it even worse image than anything the pop-culture can do; and also because, so far, I don't risk any discrimination or anything liek that, even when coming out of the broom closet.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1233-ur-than","name":"Ur-Than","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1233_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"253"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-09-11T10:08:47-07:00","id":1459667,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ur-Than</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1452173-cultural-appropriation-in-gaming?p=1459663#post1459663\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">At least it gets treated in a more complex way than here in Europe...</div> </div> </div> </div>It isn't. Their view on Rio de Janeiro is anything but complex. And their view on the Amazon don't include that there are people living in South America. Not few think that Rio de Janeiro is basically an out in the open giant brothel, and the frequency of american tourists being caught naked in public or sexually assaulting people is astounding, so this is very much a prejudice with horrible consequences.<br /> <br /> <br /> And yeah, I know how you feel about pop-culture treatment of several pantheons, I'm a Neo-Pagan myself, too. Gods of death have it particularly rough given how they are conflated with notions of death and/or underworld that have nothing to do with them...","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-09-11T13:28:54-07:00","id":1459685,"title":"","content":"The WoD's treatment of South America is also so heavily skewed by WtA, it's hard to see some of the better stuff from Mage and Wraith that acknowledge there's a lot more to the continent than Rio and the rain forest.<br /> <br /> We're also in the CofD forum, which generally does... less worse.... than the WoD when it comes to South America. CofD 2e's switch to shorter write-ups of lots of cities to show whatever character type (since this is done for broad like Vampires, and more specific like Covenants) varying by local cultural impact means they can't just do Rio over and over. Though South America and Africa are still frustratingly unrepresented in comparison. The lack of a hidden global supernatural war over the environment also means there's nothing like the Amazon War to steal the spotlight from more locally focused stories.<br /> <br /> While I don't think anyone living in South America needs to specifically step up, I do think there's a struggle to find people that know the local areas well and want to be RPG writers. Brazil is the only South American country I'm aware of with a very sizeable WoD/CofD fan base that leads to some people thinking about submitting to write on the books. Which ends up with a Catch-22 between trying to represent a more diverse setting, and not having a few people in the US or Europe writing about Uruguay based on what they find on Google.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-09-11T14:12:29-07:00","id":1459691,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1452173-cultural-appropriation-in-gaming?p=1459667#post1459667\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It isn't. Their view on Rio de Janeiro is anything but complex. And their view on the Amazon don't include that there are people living in South America. Not few think that Rio de Janeiro is basically an out in the open giant brothel, and <b>the frequency of american tourists being caught naked in public or sexually assaulting people is astounding</b>, so this is very much a prejudice with horrible consequences.<br /> <br /> <br /> And yeah, I know how you feel about pop-culture treatment of several pantheons, I'm a Neo-Pagan myself, too. Gods of death have it particularly rough given how they are conflated with notions of death and/or underworld that have nothing to do with them...</div> </div> </div> </div>For the bold part : -_- Seriously ? What are those people thinking !?<br /> <br /> Regarding the Amazon... it stems from the original creation of National Park like Yellowstone and Yosemite as a mean to reduce even more Native territories I think, since it's a model which has been imported almost all over the world (France is one of the few countries were it wasn't used because moving populations outside the protected areas was too much of an hassle... and a good way to have fights politicians didn't want). It also creates a lot of problems because it has been now proven that natives living in what became National Parks the world over actually maintained a healthier ecosystem and reduced poaching since those peoples didn't need it to supplement their lives which are at the bottom of the barrel in &quot;modern&quot; societies...<br /> <br /> Regarding Death gods... I'm still hoping for ONE pop-culture creation which doesn't conflate Hades with the Devil (or which doesn't sink to the level of The Clash of Titans from 2010 where me and my friends, seeing the Djiin, correctly deduced that they'd go all suicide bomber on someone/something at some point, as soon as we saw them because that movie is a cursed insult to everything it touches. But I digress).<br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar617_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"617\" data-vbnamecard=\"617\">Heavy Arms</a> : I'm currently a player in a WtF campaign set in Guatemala City, and our GM is actually from Guatemala City (he was born and spent a large part of his childhood and teenage years there at least) and it's just awesome. Since all the players are French I'm not sure we are making it justice, but it definitively feels like he knows the place.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1233,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1233-ur-than","user":"Ur-Than","date":". 09-11-2021, 05:15 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1233-ur-than","name":"Ur-Than","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1233_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"253"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-09-11T14:50:23-07:00","id":1459697,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ur-Than</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1452173-cultural-appropriation-in-gaming?p=1459691#post1459691\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">For the bold part : -_- Seriously ? What are those people thinking !?</div> </div> </div> </div>The preceding part is literal. How can someone be so obtuse? Your guess' as good as mine.<br /> <br /> Conflating Hades with the Devil is the usual, but what really surprised me was an episode of a super hero series (some time ago, don't remember which) where Osiris was conflated to the Devil, then as Isis is his wife they made her the villain that wanted to clash the world of the living with the underworld to bring him here...","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-09-11T23:56:35-07:00","id":1459729,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1452173-cultural-appropriation-in-gaming?p=1459697#post1459697\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Conflating Hades with the Devil is the usual, but what really surprised me was an episode of a super hero series (some time ago, don't remember which) where Osiris was conflated to the Devil, then as Isis is his wife they made her the villain that wanted to clash the world of the living with the underworld to bring him here...</div> </div> </div> </div>Well... now that's something ! Especially when the Egyptian Pantheon has Apophis and to a lesser degree Seth evil gods/godlike entities.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1233-ur-than","name":"Ur-Than","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1233_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"253"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-09-12T03:14:49-07:00","id":1459739,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ur-Than</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1452173-cultural-appropriation-in-gaming?p=1459691#post1459691\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Regarding Death gods... I'm still hoping for ONE pop-culture creation which doesn't conflate Hades with the Devil (or which doesn't sink to the level of The Clash of Titans from 2010 where me and my friends, seeing the Djiin, correctly deduced that they'd go all suicide bomber on someone/something at some point, as soon as we saw them because that movie is a cursed insult to everything it touches. But I digress).</div> </div> </div> </div>Hades, the game from Supergiant Games. He's still the main antagonist, but because of highly personal reasons rather than any notion of Christian evil/sin or hate for the living or anything like that, and it is partway through the game revealed that Zeus is ultimately the larger asshole (which is highly in tone with actual Greek mythology).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Writer for <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/410713/Bloodlines-The-Ageless\" target=\"_blank\">Bloodlines: The Ageless</a> and <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/491569/Fire--Revolution-Carthians\" target=\"_blank\">Fire &amp; Revolution: Carthians</a> on STV<br /> Some other stuff I've done: Ordo Dracul Mysteries: <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1138490-idea-coil-of-owls?p=1140522\" target=\"_blank\">Mystery of Smoke</a>, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1227848-mystery-of-%C5%BEiva-house-rule-revision\" target=\"_blank\">Revised Mystery of \u017diva</a> Mage The Awakening: <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/0B2LMDRtfCcpdZDU1dWJzT1dNeEE/view\" target=\"_blank\">Spell Quick Reference</a> (single page and landscape for computer screens)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11887-tessie","name":"Tessie","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11887_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4406"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-09-14T11:36:26-07:00","id":1459951,"title":"","content":"Hades in the rebooted <i>Ducktales</i> series is also not conflated with the Devil. He doesn't get too much screentime due to being a minor character, but he's pretty hilarious, especially since his design seems to be based on Alice Cooper.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26495-shinsoryuu","name":"Shinsoryuu","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26495_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"28"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-10-04T11:31:48-07:00","id":1462680,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ur-Than</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1452173-cultural-appropriation-in-gaming?p=1459663#post1459663\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Regarding cultural appropriation, I think the main difference between it and culture heritage between cultures is often that it's a majority group taking something which used to be (and is often still) depicted as something dirty or &amp;quot;primitive&amp;quot; etc.</div> </div> </div> </div>I would say that is a very good starting point in terms of where to draw a line in the sand.<br /> <br /> But something I've encountered and find to be much more concerning is the...I think the term is &quot;orientalizing&quot;? --of other cultures. Portraying the differences between them and yours as &quot;exotic&quot;.<br /> <br /> On one hand, I hear things about other cultures and I'm like &quot;what? That's awesome! I want to include that in a story/setting/game and show other people this awesome thing!&quot;, but it's just...so easy to go from that to essentially putting the other culture in a museum or a zoo to be oooh'd at.<br /> That's why I've sort of focused some of my efforts regarding this discussion to accuracy. Trying to portray the culture in an accurate and complete way, rather than as something in a curiosity cabinet.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26844-quixote","name":"Quixote","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"40"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-10-04T16:00:21-07:00","id":1462726,"title":"","content":"To me the main point is to let the natives speak for themselves as much as possible.<br /> <br /> Why K-pop and manga aren't considered a case of cultural appropriation? Because they didn't got popular through white interpretations with original artists remaining &quot;obscure&quot;. Instead most famous K-pop bands (you like them or not) the world around are, well, Korean. And most famous manga and anime are Japanese. There is a lot of other problems, like &quot;orientalization&quot;, and certainly there are instances where we have a strong chain of cultural appropriation (anyone here ever heard about or played a wuxia, samurai, or &quot;oriental&quot; setting written by Asians?), but those two specific cases are ones where we actually traded with the natives themselves.<br /> <br /> Cultural appropriation is all around us in elements from other cultures that we actually only know through the view of our &quot;equals&quot;, and in that we actually just re-skin our own cultures and world views. When we learn then primarily through natives, then we have a space for true cultural exchange.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-10-08T11:12:47-07:00","id":1463124,"title":"","content":"Curious Cultural appropriation is the fact I a caucasion who has never left the united states in my entire adult life (Spent 3 years of my childhood in germany with Daddy) am a Registered Member of Tsubaki Jinja washington <br /> <br /> <br /> Most because I lost my faith in christianity but dont look good skyclad<br /> \\<br /> <br /> Is that cultural appropriation<br /> <br /> I Found it synched with the devotion t o environmentalism I a llready had.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://imagizer.imageshack.us/a/img829/1897/8seg.png\" target=\"_blank\"><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imagizer.imageshack.us/a/img829/1897/8seg.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imageshack.com/i/2iw20supportergarou125p\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imageshack.com/i/0j5hz8j\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /></a><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imageshack.com/i/idnSdTJkj\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://i.imgsafe.org/97fd805379.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1153-prince-of-the-night","name":"Prince of the Night","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4205"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-10-08T14:10:25-07:00","id":1463153,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Prince of the Night</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1452173-cultural-appropriation-in-gaming?p=1463124#post1463124\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Is that cultural appropriation</div> </div> </div> </div>Nope. \u693f\u5927\u795e\u793e is a Japanese shrine and the American one is just a branch. The institution is Japanese and the shrine was founded and kept by them. The current head priest isn't Japanese but was pointed by the Japanese branch all the same.<br /> <br /> It would be cultural appropriation if you were following shinto interpretations created by Americans without consulting the original followers. That's not the case at all.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-10-09T21:19:55-07:00","id":1463281,"title":"","content":"Found I Had neither the wealth nor the patience to keep a proper Kamidana I Leave it up there mostly as a statement of faith/Decoration","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://imagizer.imageshack.us/a/img829/1897/8seg.png\" target=\"_blank\"><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imagizer.imageshack.us/a/img829/1897/8seg.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imageshack.com/i/2iw20supportergarou125p\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imageshack.com/i/0j5hz8j\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /></a><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imageshack.com/i/idnSdTJkj\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://i.imgsafe.org/97fd805379.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":1153,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1153-prince-of-the-night","user":"Prince of the Night","date":". 10-10-2021, 12:47 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1153-prince-of-the-night","name":"Prince of the Night","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4205"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-10-12T23:17:31-07:00","id":1463651,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> how do you deal with the concept that many common tropes of horror and fantasy come from real-world cultures, and how do you navigate/mitigate the consequences? </div> </div> </div>Honestly? I don't.<br /> <br /> I deal with topics that actually come up in play for my groups, and for the past twenty years I've been engaged in the hobby, this hasn't come up once.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3570-lnodiv","name":"lnodiv","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3570_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1607"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-12-07T10:00:23-08:00","id":1469851,"title":"","content":"Is it cultural appropriation for an african or asian to recite the lords Prayer?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://imagizer.imageshack.us/a/img829/1897/8seg.png\" target=\"_blank\"><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imagizer.imageshack.us/a/img829/1897/8seg.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imageshack.com/i/2iw20supportergarou125p\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imageshack.com/i/0j5hz8j\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /></a><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imageshack.com/i/idnSdTJkj\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://i.imgsafe.org/97fd805379.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1153-prince-of-the-night","name":"Prince of the Night","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4205"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-12-07T13:09:06-08:00","id":1469865,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Prince of the Night</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1452173-cultural-appropriation-in-gaming?p=1469851#post1469851\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Is it cultural appropriation for an african or asian to recite the lords Prayer?</div> </div> </div> </div>No. As in your case with Shinto, they have their own branches of Christianity to follow thanks to missionaries. Currently they are completely Asian branches, but they're born from native teachings.<br /> <br /> But in general it is hard to talk about Cultural Appropriation when it relates to following religions (as opposed to just depicting those religions in media). So it isn't CA to follow a religion from another culture or region, but it may be to depict this religion in a fiction book without input from natives.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-12-07T14:53:10-08:00","id":1469873,"title":"","content":"Another important thing to note, regarding say the Lord\u2019s Prayer, is that most forms of Christianity are closed in only the loosest of ways. The stricter sects may require a period of education before the rites of initiation can be performed but plenty of sects consider accepting Jesus of Nazareth as Christ within your heart to be perfectly sufficient to become a Christian.<br /> <br /> That\u2019s leaving aside the historical power dynamics which come into play with Christianity having been used as a tool of European colonial and imperial power for centuries. <br /> <br /> Certain practices and faiths are closed and respect for the practitioners and their spiritual truths obligate outsiders to respect that the faith is closed and that it should not be used by outsiders except in whatever ways are specifically allowed by the practitioners. Open practices or practices that are nearly open, such as Christianity or Islam for example - which both have strong proselytizing traditions as well as relatively easy initiation procedures, are going to be more flexible in how outsiders can interact with their practices although I still feel that it\u2019s important to offer respect and dignity to the practitioners and their spiritual mysteries.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11637-gryffon15","name":"Gryffon15","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11637_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"900"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-12-07T16:05:32-08:00","id":1469881,"title":"","content":"It's worth considering that cultural appropriation is about cultures, not places (obviously cultures can hold specific values regarding specific locations though).<br /> <br /> The Lord's Prayer originated in Asia, and spread to Africa as fast as it did to Europe. It's fair to think of it as culturally European due to the influence of Hellenism and the Pax Romana in the parts of Asia and Africa where Christianity originated and spread, but Coptic Christians are one of the oldest known groups of Christians, and most certainly aren't from Europe. And they definitely have their version of the Lord's Prayer.<br /> <br /> IME, a lot of people from Africa and Asia find it kind of weird that the &quot;African&quot; and &quot;Asian&quot; are used with such unspoken assumptions about what cultures and peoples are being discussed. I know Moroccan, Ethiopian, and South African immigrants to the US, and all of them have struggled with the idea that they're not all Africans in the US; even though Morocco, Ethiopia, and South Africa are all in Africa. Moroccans get treated as Middle Eastern, and the South Africans as European. &quot;African = black,&quot; and &quot;Asian = south east Asia,&quot; is understandable to an extent as a functional shorthand in many countries, but it's worth thinking about such assumptions when diving deeper into issues like cultural appropriation.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-12-07T19:37:07-08:00","id":1469894,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>DontEatRawHagis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1452173-cultural-appropriation-in-gaming?p=1452464#post1452464\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Some stuff has become a bit too blatantly used, but in regards to skin-walkers in particular, I understand, the Navajo nation does not like discussing them with outsiders. And as such it feels wrong for me to use in my games. I've also started to get a bit more sensitive when I see Native American culture being reflavored as mysticism within media. And man are there a lot of Urban Fantasy books where one of the main characters is a Native American who can shapeshift or has a spirit animal.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"b-bbcode b-bbcode__url b-bbcode__url--preview\"> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Skin-walker\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"urlimg_container\"> <img class=\"js-img-fallback\" src=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/static/images/icons/enwiki-25.svg\"> </a> <div class=\"urlinfo\"> <div class=\"title ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Skin-walker\" target=\"_blank\"> Skin-walker - Wikipedia </a> </div> <div class=\"url ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Skin-walker\" target=\"_blank\"> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Skin-walker </a> </div> <div class=\"meta\"> </div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> That being said some times I'll take inspiration from folklore, but I'll not do a 1 to 1 representation and I'll not use the name.</div> </div> </div> </div>A bit late but I try to lean on european werewolf folk lore were one dons a pelt to shape shift in these situations for those reasons you list","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2909-mr-f-i-x","name":"Mr.F.I.X.","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2909_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1053"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-12-08T04:07:58-08:00","id":1469902,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Mr.F.I.X.</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1452173-cultural-appropriation-in-gaming?p=1469894#post1469894\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">A bit late but I try to lean on european werewolf folk lore were one dons a pelt to shape shift in these situations for those reasons you list</div> </div> </div> </div>Even in Europe there's a lot of variety on shapechangers. They're too common a trope in folklore world-wide to be necessary to rely specifically on Navajo lore they don't feel comfortable sharing that way.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-12-08T06:14:39-08:00","id":1469910,"title":"","content":"Currently I'm having an issue with Mummy in regards to Irem relying on the Egyptian themes. Although I do like Mummy's theme of reclaiming the Relics of Irem as a metaphor for cultural appropriation and Western countries stealing from other countries cultural sites. That being said I'm not sure how I'd run mummy without Irem just being a renamed version of Ancient Egypt.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25291-donteatrawhagis","name":"DontEatRawHagis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25291_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"211"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-12-08T07:19:33-08:00","id":1469914,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>DontEatRawHagis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1452173-cultural-appropriation-in-gaming?p=1469910#post1469910\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Currently I'm having an issue with Mummy in regards to Irem relying on the Egyptian themes. Although I do like Mummy's theme of reclaiming the Relics of Irem as a metaphor for cultural appropriation and Western countries stealing from other countries cultural sites. That being said I'm not sure how I'd run mummy without Irem just being a renamed version of Ancient Egypt.</div> </div> </div> </div>Does it help at all that a lot of the original writers for Mummy were Egyptian people?<br /> <br /> I'll point out that the Nameless Empire isn't meant to be a stand-in for or based off of ancient Egypt, it's fiction drawing on the fact that the origins of the cultural continuity that we regard as ancient Egypt (which itself lasted for ages) are incredibly old, and end up stretching back to a time that becomes very obscure.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-12-08T10:14:25-08:00","id":1469929,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1452173-cultural-appropriation-in-gaming?p=1469914#post1469914\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Does it help at all that a lot of the original writers for Mummy were Egyptian people?</div> </div> </div> </div>That's really cool to know!<br /> <br /> But also, it's good to keep in mind that Ancient Egypt isn't the same thing as Modern Egypt. Many of the cultural references about it are things already out of practice during the Ptolemaic Dynasty, and Egypt underwent many more cultural transformations since.<br /> <br /> Not that it doesn't have an impact on them, but we shouldn't expect to aggrieve modern Egyptians as if they were ancient Egyptians. Instead, it is good to recognize that Egypt exist today as its own thing (and not just as the outpost White Explorers use to go to pyramids).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-12-08T13:09:54-08:00","id":1469951,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1452173-cultural-appropriation-in-gaming?p=1469902#post1469902\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Even in Europe there's a lot of variety on shapechangers. They're too common a trope in folklore world-wide to be necessary to rely specifically on Navajo lore they don't feel comfortable sharing that way.</div> </div> </div> </div>Oh for sure and if anything that's my point. By leaning on other folk lore sources I just mean that I can deal with my personal opinions and/feelings of running a monster based on such instead of having it be referred to as a skin walker in game.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2909-mr-f-i-x","name":"Mr.F.I.X.","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2909_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1053"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-12-08T16:55:01-08:00","id":1469975,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1452173-cultural-appropriation-in-gaming?p=1469929#post1469929\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> Not that it doesn't have an impact on them, but we shouldn't expect to aggrieve modern Egyptians as if they were ancient Egyptians.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think the country of Egypt having had some form of a Ministry of Antiquities for the last several decades dedicated to the return of artefacts taken under imperialist auspices suggests that modern Egyptians feel some degree of ownership over that ancient heritage, which would still have included lineal ancestors for modern people in the area. <br /> <br /> It's enough that I think there are people with that as their cultural background, and possibly with some dedicated scholarship, who are worth consulting to help ensure engagement with the subject has a bit more understanding of the lifestyles and value systems of actual people, rather than using the ancient heritage as salacious window dressing.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-12-08T17:16:59-08:00","id":1469979,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1452173-cultural-appropriation-in-gaming?p=1469975#post1469975\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think the country of Egypt having had some form of a Ministry of Antiquities for the last several decades dedicated to the return of artefacts taken under imperialist auspices suggests that modern Egyptians feel some degree of ownership over that ancient heritage, which would still have included lineal ancestors for modern people in the area.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yes, but my point is the way you approach the matter. I'm not saying to not care about sensible approach to Ancient Egypt, but to not forget in the process that there's a Modern Egypt that is its own thing.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-12-09T03:11:09-08:00","id":1470018,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1452173-cultural-appropriation-in-gaming?p=1469979#post1469979\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm not saying to not care about sensible approach to Ancient Egypt, but to not forget in the process that there's a Modern Egypt that is its own thing.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm not sure if that relates specifically to the game of Mummy: the Curse, with its premise of inhabitants of pre-dynastic Egypt periodically returning to life to perform occult missions and having been spread across the world.<br /> <br /> Is one of the Second Edition sample settings in modern Egypt?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-07-16T14:40:55-07:00","id":1561783,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Neos01</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1452173-cultural-appropriation-in-gaming?p=1452752#post1452752\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Apart from the legend of the Golem of Prague i did not knew anything else existed about Golem s &quot;legend&quot;.<br /> Now i understand your discomfort, it s akin to open a monster manual and seeing &quot;evil human fetus&quot; showed as a mindless flesh eating enemy (at least for me, i would feel in a similar way i think).<br /> Thanks for sharing it.</div> </div> </div> </div>I believe the word <i>&quot;golem&quot;</i> also might translate to <i>&quot;half formed?&quot;</i><br /> <br /> Which makes a lot more sense with this information putting it into context.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/29009-darkking","name":"DarkKing","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar29009_1.png","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2025"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"639"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-07-16T15:30:44-07:00","id":1561786,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1452173-cultural-appropriation-in-gaming?p=1470018#post1470018\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Is one of the Second Edition sample settings in modern Egypt?</div> </div> </div> </div>Don't remember right now, but the game does imply that they awaken slightly more frequently there.<br /> <br /> For whatever it's worth, what I learned since that exchange:<br /> <br /> Item would more or less equate to the Egyptian prehistory. 6k years ago is about the time the proto-dynasties start to form, so Item's end is an implication that pre-dynastic Egypt didn't quite existed, but from that point on history is correct.<br /> <br /> Egyptians are the same ethnicity to this day. They're mostly culturally arabized, which is a very particular process, but otherwise both the people and a lot of its culture is in direct line of continuity from proto-dynastic Egypt. This is known and celebrated, and evidence points to many things like instruments and cuisine being reasonably well preserved from ancient past.<br /> <br /> Religion is the obvious exception, although they don't feel ashamed by the religion of their ancestors or something. Coptic Christians also preserved a direct evolution of their ancient language.<br /> <br /> Overall it doesn't seems that Irem steps on any modern Egyptian toes, since the one thing it gamefies is the one thing they don't have any religious attachment and doesn't contradict the actual traditions they care about.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>DarkKing</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1452173-cultural-appropriation-in-gaming?p=1561783#post1561783\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I believe the word <i>&quot;golem&quot;</i> also might translate to <i>&quot;half formed?&quot;</i></div> </div> </div> </div>The literal translation is a formless lump of clay, but the idea is indeed that you have an effigy being made, but not yet formed.<br /> <br /> In texts like the &quot;bible&quot;, the Talmud and Sefer Yetzirah it's always used to refer to a human in ignorance or, specifically for Adam, before the Breath of Life.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-07-16T17:05:44-07:00","id":1561792,"title":"","content":"Half formed doesn't really fit. The root stem G-L-M means to gather, fold, or collect. Keeping in mind that ancient Hebrew was not written with vowels and other issues with precise translations, in the noun form of &quot;golem&quot; most closely means, &quot;material gathered for a purpose.&quot; You wouldn't call the raw unformed clay out in nature a &quot;golem.&quot; It becomes that once someone has gathered it up and gotten it ready to be used. This is why the earliest recorded use (in Psalms, which a mess when it comes to dating) is usually translated as &quot;substance.&quot; The psalmist specifically says golemi, or &quot;my substance,&quot; as the poem in question is praising the creation of the human from body to mind and spirit.<br /> <br /> That's way formless is generally better than half-formed. Though you need to be careful to keep the connotation of intent to form. It doesn't literally mean a specific material though, the clay association comes from the connection to Adam. While we don't have anything in the noun form, verbal forms are using in other context in extra-biblical sources. A fragmentary line about &quot;golem&quot; some wool seems to clearly be talking about preparing to make yarn, not applying clay to sheared fur.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-07-16T19:20:04-07:00","id":1561802,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1452173-cultural-appropriation-in-gaming?p=1561786#post1561786\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> In texts like the &quot;bible&quot;, the Talmud and Sefer Yetzirah it's always used to refer to a human in ignorance or, <b>specifically for Adam</b>, before the Breath of Life.</div> </div> </div> </div>Actually, that reference does sound familiar. I know I've heard it before, but it must have been a very long time ago.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/29009-darkking","name":"DarkKing","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar29009_1.png","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2025"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"639"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-07-16T20:51:02-07:00","id":1561803,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1452173-cultural-appropriation-in-gaming?p=1561792#post1561792\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It doesn't literally mean a specific material though, the clay association comes from the connection to Adam. While we don't have anything in the noun form, verbal forms are using in other context in extra-biblical sources. A fragmentary line about &quot;golem&quot; some wool seems to clearly be talking about preparing to make yarn, not applying clay to sheared fur.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's good to know. The sources I could find were unanimous about being clay, but while they were Jewish, they weren't scholars talking to scholars, but someone talking to the US internet.<br /> <br /> I hate index localization. Because of that I need to VPN myself to Tel Aviv if I want decent results for basic questions (or N other places).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>DarkKing</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1452173-cultural-appropriation-in-gaming?p=1561802#post1561802\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Actually, that reference does sound familiar. I know I've heard it before, but it must have been a very long time ago.</div> </div> </div> </div>It's in the Talmud, if that helps, a rabbinic text, not part of any Christian bible. The bible has only the one in Psalms, the Talmud has this reference to Adam in the &quot;first 12 hours of formation&quot;, distinguishing before and after the Breath of Life. The Sefer Yetzirah goes in length about the creation of a golem, though it's meaning is debatable and most likely metaphor and maybe an instruction for meditation.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-07-16T21:57:53-07:00","id":1561805,"title":"","content":"The non-clay thing was probably &quot;archaic&quot; by ~500 or 400 BCE. It seems like a very rare word until it became more popular in theological use. It is kinda weird to think about a 3,000 year old language as one that has changed many many times during it's original height of use even though we all know how much language can change in our own lifetimes. It doesn't help that it transitioned into a liturgical language which meant it stagnated for along time before it's more recent modernization.<br /> <br /> There are some good sources you don't need VPNs for, but they're all paywalled academic references... and ones that are hard to use if you don't have a lot of training.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}